{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jax3gu88NmieDCF5aWeqAS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already has data: 009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected NVE peak 2.05x. Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 131,\n7\t      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 4.12,\n8\t      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": 55000,\n9\t      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n10\t      \"seed1_collapse_step\": 55000,\n11\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 268,\n12\t      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 2.05,\n13\t      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": 25000,\n14\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n15\t      \"seed2_self_correction_nve_105K\": 0.38,\n16\t      \"both_seeds_exceed_1.5x\": true,\n17\t      \"threshold_separating_power\": \"2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\",\n18\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (stochastic)\"\n19\t    },\n20\t    \"tables\": [\n21\t      {\n22\t        \"name\": \"NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\",\n23\t        \"columns\": [\n24\t          \"step\",\n25\t          \"seed1_nve\",\n26\t          \"seed1_eval\",\n27\t          \"seed2_nve\",\n28\t          \"seed2_eval\"\n29\t        ],\n30\t        \"rows\": [\n31\t          [\n32\t            25000,\n33\t            1.78,\n34\t            167,\n35\t            2.05,\n36\t            141\n37\t          ],\n38\t          [\n39\t            35000,\n40\t            1.71,\n41\t            146,\n42\t            1.24,\n43\t            164\n44\t          ],\n45\t          [\n46\t            45000,\n47\t            2.09,\n48\t            141,\n49\t            1.62,\n50\t            141\n51\t          ],\n52\t          [\n53\t            55000,\n54\t            4.12,\n55\t            83,\n56\t            1.41,\n57\t            172\n58\t          ],\n59\t          [\n60\t            65000,\n61\t            1.23,\n62\t            250,\n63\t            0.94,\n64\t            209\n65\t          ],\n66\t          [\n67\t            75000,\n68\t            1.48,\n69\t            178,\n70\t            0.66,\n71\t            197\n72\t          ],\n73\t          [\n74\t            85000,\n75\t            1.15,\n76\t            202,\n77\t            0.51,\n78\t            236\n79\t          ],\n80\t          [\n81\t            95000,\n82\t            1.73,\n83\t            133,\n84\t            0.43,\n85\t            279\n86\t          ],\n87\t          [\n88\t            105000,\n89\t            1.57,\n90\t            131,\n91\t            0.38,\n92\t            268\n93\t          ]\n94\t        ]\n95\t      }\n96\t    ],\n97\t    \"figures\": [],\n98\t    \"analysis\": \"Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds exceed NVE 1.5x, maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across trained/untrained combined). Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary condition for CIVO, not sufficient for collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence distinguishes the two outcomes.\\n\\n\\nTrained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap 2.78x) is amplified through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained dt_emb reliably enters the pathological NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is deterministic — the dt_emb learns to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this differentiation against inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse) or transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer sampling order, initialization, and whether the actor's policy gradient from real returns can counteract the critic's overestimate before it locks in (primacy bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\\n\"\n99\t  },\n100\t  {\n101\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\",\n102\t    \"description\": \"Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra). Seed 2 (009c) ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best tier, seed 1 re-run shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing 1.5x.\\n\",\n103\t    \"results\": {\n104\t      \"seed1_rerun_eval_105K\": 420,\n105\t      \"seed1_rerun_nve_peak\": 0.95,\n106\t      \"seed1_rerun_nve_105K\": 0.35,\n107\t      \"seed1_rerun_collapse\": false,\n108\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 148,\n109\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 1.14,\n110\t      \"seed2_eval_200K\": 280,\n111\t      \"seed2_nve_200K\": 0.85,\n112\t      \"seed2_nve_peak_ever\": 1.26,\n113\t      \"seed2_crosses_1.5x\": false,\n114\t      \"both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus\": true,\n115\t      \"threshold_separating_power\": \"0/2 exceed 1.5x\"\n116\t    },\n117\t    \"tables\": [\n118\t      {\n119\t        \"name\": \"Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\",\n120\t        \"columns\": [\n121\t          \"step\",\n122\t          \"eval_return\",\n123\t          \"nve\",\n124\t          \"note\"\n125\t        ],\n126\t        \"rows\": [\n127\t          [\n128\t            105000,\n129\t            148,\n130\t            1.14,\n131\t            \"actual\"\n132\t          ],\n133\t          [\n134\t            125000,\n135\t            195,\n136\t            1.05,\n137\t            \"value still adjusting\"\n138\t          ],\n139\t          [\n140\t            145000,\n141\t            240,\n142\t            0.92,\n143\t            \"value correction begins\"\n144\t          ],\n145\t          [\n146\t            165000,\n147\t            270,\n148\t            0.88,\n149\t            \"eval recovering\"\n150\t          ],\n151\t          [\n152\t            185000,\n153\t            290,\n154\t            0.85,\n155\t            \"stabilizing\"\n156\t          ],\n157\t          [\n158\t            200000,\n159\t            280,\n160\t            0.85,\n161\t            \"plateau\"\n162\t          ]\n163\t        ]\n164\t      }\n165\t    ],\n166\t    \"figures\": [],\n167\t    \"analysis\": \"Seed 2's decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO — NVE never exceeded 1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different (slow value tracking vs value explosion). The 200K extension confirms plateau without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to show healthier zigzag recovery (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for C2: 0/2 untrained seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined with plan_011.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning information. The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 2.78x with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback loop cannot form. Seed 2's rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K) while eval declined, but this is fundamentally different from CIVO: the value is simply slow to track a declining eval, not systematically overestimating. Extended to 200K, the value function adjusts downward as it accumulates more accurate return estimates, pulling NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K), because the underlying mechanism is the same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\\n\"\n168\t  },\n169\t  {\n170\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\",\n171\t    \"description\": \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done: seed 1 success (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\\n\",\n172\t    \"results\": {\n173\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 869.9,\n174\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.8,\n175\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n176\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 137,\n177\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 3.78,\n178\t      \"seed2_collapse\": true,\n179\t      \"seed2_pathway\": \"B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\",\n180\t      \"seed2_actor_entropy_collapse\": \"-5.21_to_-3.83\",\n181\t      \"pathway_b_rate_walker\": \"1/2\",\n182\t      \"pathway_a_blocked\": true,\n183\t      \"gate_dt_mean_both_seeds\": 1.0\n184\t    },\n185\t    \"tables\": [\n186\t      {\n187\t        \"name\": \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\",\n188\t        \"columns\": [\n189\t          \"feature\",\n190\t          \"pathway_a_008d_s1\",\n191\t          \"pathway_b_008e_v3_s2\"\n192\t        ],\n193\t        \"rows\": [\n194\t          [\n195\t            \"value change at collapse\",\n196\t            \"+130% explosion\",\n197\t            \"+13% mild\"\n198\t          ],\n199\t          [\n200\t            \"eval change at collapse\",\n201\t            \"-43% follows value\",\n202\t            \"-81% leads\"\n203\t          ],\n204\t          [\n205\t            \"NVE driver\",\n206\t            \"value numerator\",\n207\t            \"eval denominator\"\n208\t          ],\n209\t          [\n210\t            \"actor_entropy\",\n211\t            \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\",\n212\t            \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\"\n213\t          ],\n214\t          [\n215\t            \"mechanism\",\n216\t            \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\",\n217\t            \"eval crash → value lag\"\n218\t          ]\n219\t        ]\n220\t      }\n221\t    ],\n222\t    \"figures\": [],\n223\t    \"analysis\": \"C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination eliminates the more understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes a contribution rather than a weakness — the paper demonstrates that surface-level mitigations are insufficient.\\n\\n\\ndt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the critic never evaluates dt>1 imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback loop requires dt>1 predictions where RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained dt_embedding still receives gradient from observation decoders during world model training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes dt=2 in training data). This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific information that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses only dt=1. Seed 2's collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137) with increasing entropy (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has decreasing entropy). The policy destabilizes because the world model's latent space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb, not because the critic directly overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it because its specific initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by the dt_emb gradients.\\n\"\n224\t  },\n225\t  {\n226\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\",\n227\t    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional seeds. Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery cycles.\\n\",\n228\t    \"results\": {\n229\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 720,\n230\t      \"seed1_nve_range\": [\n231\t        0.55,\n232\t        0.9\n233\t      ],\n234\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n235\t      \"seed1_v_recovery_count\": 2,\n236\t      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n237\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 690,\n238\t      \"seed2_nve_range\": [\n239\t        0.5,\n240\t        0.95\n241\t      ],\n242\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.58,\n243\t      \"seed2_v_recovery_count\": 1,\n244\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n245\t      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 729,\n246\t      \"cross_seed_mean_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n247\t      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-20% (range -15% to -25%)\"\n248\t    },\n249\t    \"tables\": [\n250\t      {\n251\t        \"name\": \"Cross-seed V-recovery summary\",\n252\t        \"columns\": [\n253\t          \"seed\",\n254\t          \"eval_55K\",\n255\t          \"eval_65K\",\n256\t          \"eval_85K\",\n257\t          \"eval_105K\",\n258\t          \"nve_55K\",\n259\t          \"nve_105K\"\n260\t        ],\n261\t        \"rows\": [\n262\t          [\n263\t            0,\n264\t            488,\n265\t            608,\n266\t            584,\n267\t            778,\n268\t            0.93,\n269\t            0.59\n270\t          ],\n271\t          [\n272\t            1,\n273\t            450,\n274\t            570,\n275\t            620,\n276\t            720,\n277\t            0.85,\n278\t            0.62\n279\t          ],\n280\t          [\n281\t            2,\n282\t            420,\n283\t            540,\n284\t            560,\n285\t            690,\n286\t            0.9,\n287\t            0.58\n288\t          ]\n289\t        ]\n290\t      }\n291\t    ],\n292\t    \"figures\": [],\n293\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection. The ±10% eval variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3's typical seed variability. The key invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent with the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\\n\\n\\nUntrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees dt>1 data) means the embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination encounters dt>1 predictions, the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning for those values. The critic cannot form dt-specific overestimates because the dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information. NVE stays structurally suppressed (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the quality of dt=1-only imagination. The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic exploration-exploitation oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative. New seeds show similar pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration stochasticity), but the NVE suppression is robust because it's a structural property of the unexposed dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\\n\"\n294\t  },\n295\t  {\n296\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\",\n297\t    \"description\": \"Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0 (gradient starvation), eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\\n\",\n298\t    \"results\": {\n299\t      \"eval_105K\": 204,\n300\t      \"nve_range\": [\n301\t        0.39,\n302\t        0.47\n303\t      ],\n304\t      \"nve_105K\": 0.42,\n305\t      \"actor_loss_105K\": 0.000179,\n306\t      \"actor_entropy_105K\": -4.855,\n307\t      \"collapse\": false,\n308\t      \"civo_detected\": false,\n309\t      \"gradient_starvation\": true,\n310\t      \"gate_dt_mean\": 2.0\n311\t    },\n312\t    \"tables\": [\n313\t      {\n314\t        \"name\": \"2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\",\n315\t        \"columns\": [\n316\t          \"config\",\n317\t          \"dt_emb_status\",\n318\t          \"nve_peak_35K_plus\",\n319\t          \"collapse\",\n320\t          \"failure_mode\"\n321\t        ],\n322\t        \"rows\": [\n323\t          [\n324\t            \"008d\",\n325\t            \"trained (grad ON)\",\n326\t            \"4.12x\",\n327\t            \"yes (1/2)\",\n328\t            \"CIVO\"\n329\t          ],\n330\t          [\n331\t            \"008f\",\n332\t            \"absent (dim=0)\",\n333\t            \"1.26x\",\n334\t            \"no\",\n335\t            \"reward degradation\"\n336\t          ],\n337\t          [\n338\t            \"015b\",\n339\t            \"frozen (grad OFF)\",\n340\t            \"0.47x\",\n341\t            \"no\",\n342\t            \"gradient starvation\"\n343\t          ],\n344\t          [\n345\t            \"008c\",\n346\t            \"untrained (subsample OFF)\",\n347\t            \"0.90x\",\n348\t            \"no\",\n349\t            \"learning signal reduction\"\n350\t          ]\n351\t        ]\n352\t      }\n353\t    ],\n354\t    \"figures\": [],\n355\t    \"analysis\": \"015b closes the \\\"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\\\" question definitively. The 2×2 matrix shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x. All other configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms — this is the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \\\"trained dt_emb is bad\\\" to \\\"the gradient pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific vulnerability.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFrozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant random conditioning per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments → NVE stays far below 1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb. However, the frozen random embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic system: the actor receives confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient signal vanishes), and the gate locks to dt=2 100% because the gate network never receives informative gradients to adjust. This produces a distinct failure mode — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient starvation from persistent noise injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for trained) is the clean separation: frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible. This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix: trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO, frozen+present (015b)→no CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition.\\n\"\n356\t  },\n357\t  {\n358\t    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\",\n359\t    \"description\": \"Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok, seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4 cross-env).\\n\",\n360\t    \"results\": {\n361\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 840,\n362\t      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.78,\n363\t      \"seed3_collapse\": false,\n364\t      \"seed4_eval_105K\": 810,\n365\t      \"seed4_nve_105K\": 0.82,\n366\t      \"seed4_collapse\": false,\n367\t      \"pathway_b_rate_updated\": \"1/4 (25%)\",\n368\t      \"pathway_b_rare_confirmed\": true\n369\t    },\n370\t    \"tables\": [\n371\t      {\n372\t        \"name\": \"008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\",\n373\t        \"columns\": [\n374\t          \"seed\",\n375\t          \"eval_105K\",\n376\t          \"nve_105K\",\n377\t          \"collapse\",\n378\t          \"pathway\"\n379\t        ],\n380\t        \"rows\": [\n381\t          [\n382\t            1,\n383\t            869.9,\n384\t            0.8,\n385\t            false,\n386\t            \"-\"\n387\t          ],\n388\t          [\n389\t            2,\n390\t            137,\n391\t            3.78,\n392\t            true,\n393\t            \"B (eval-driven)\"\n394\t          ],\n395\t          [\n396\t            3,\n397\t            840,\n398\t            0.78,\n399\t            false,\n400\t            \"-\"\n401\t          ],\n402\t          [\n403\t            4,\n404\t            810,\n405\t            0.82,\n406\t            false,\n407\t            \"-\"\n408\t          ]\n409\t        ]\n410\t      }\n411\t    ],\n412\t    \"figures\": [],\n413\t    \"analysis\": \"1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \\\"dt=1 imagination eliminates the primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability affects 1/4 seeds, characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\\\" This positions dt=1 imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood mechanism while leaving a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb's latent-space corruption.\\n\\n\\nUnder best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both succeeding, the rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis that Pathway B is ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific conjunction of early training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the latent representation in a way that destabilizes the actor without triggering the classic CIVO value explosion. This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when the dt_emb gradient from observation decoders creates a latent-space distortion that specifically disrupts the actor's policy gradient, which depends on seed-specific replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid this because the distortion is mild enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes Pathway B a secondary finding — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant failure mode (CIVO, Pathway A, remains primary).\\n\"\n414\t  },\n415\t  {\n416\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\",\n417\t    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done: peak 849.8. Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\\n\",\n418\t    \"results\": {\n419\t      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 849.8,\n420\t      \"seed1_eval_500K\": 849.8,\n421\t      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 830,\n422\t      \"seed2_eval_500K\": 815,\n423\t      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 860,\n424\t      \"seed3_eval_500K\": 840,\n425\t      \"cross_seed_mean_peak\": 847,\n426\t      \"cross_seed_std\": 15,\n427\t      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.22,\n428\t      \"ve_ratio_65K\": 0.5,\n429\t      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.62,\n430\t      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\",\n431\t      \"convergence_step\": 120000\n432\t    },\n433\t    \"tables\": [\n434\t      {\n435\t        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\",\n436\t        \"columns\": [\n437\t          \"step\",\n438\t          \"seed1_ve\",\n439\t          \"seed2_ve\",\n440\t          \"seed3_ve\",\n441\t          \"mean_ve\"\n442\t        ],\n443\t        \"rows\": [\n444\t          [\n445\t            25000,\n446\t            0.09,\n447\t            0.1,\n448\t            0.08,\n449\t            0.09\n450\t          ],\n451\t          [\n452\t            35000,\n453\t            0.22,\n454\t            0.24,\n455\t            0.21,\n456\t            0.22\n457\t          ],\n458\t          [\n459\t            55000,\n460\t            0.38,\n461\t            0.4,\n462\t            0.37,\n463\t            0.38\n464\t          ],\n465\t          [\n466\t            75000,\n467\t            0.55,\n468\t            0.52,\n469\t            0.56,\n470\t            0.54\n471\t          ],\n472\t          [\n473\t            105000,\n474\t            0.62,\n475\t            0.6,\n476\t            0.64,\n477\t            0.62\n478\t          ]\n479\t        ]\n480\t      }\n481\t    ],\n482\t    \"figures\": [],\n483\t    \"analysis\": \"Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication. The v/e calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations in Cheetah Run experiments.\\n\\n\\nDreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original paper. The RSSM handles Cheetah's 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline v/e calibration should be similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because the value function learns accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate (dt=1 only). The slightly lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah Run's harder reward landscape (requires coordinated leg movements for running gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\\n\"\n484\t  },\n485\t  {\n486\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n487\t    \"description\": \"Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under best tier, NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\\n\",\n488\t    \"results\": {\n489\t      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 380,\n490\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 95,\n491\t      \"seed1_nve_35K\": 1.85,\n492\t      \"seed1_nve_45K\": 2.4,\n493\t      \"seed1_nve_55K\": 3.1,\n494\t      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n495\t      \"seed1_collapse_step\": 55000,\n496\t      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 420,\n497\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 310,\n498\t      \"seed2_nve_35K\": 1.7,\n499\t      \"seed2_nve_45K\": 1.55,\n500\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n501\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n502\t      \"seed2_self_correction\": true,\n503\t      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 350,\n504\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 120,\n505\t      \"seed3_nve_35K\": 1.9,\n506\t      \"seed3_nve_55K\": 2.8,\n507\t      \"seed3_collapse\": true,\n508\t      \"cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count\": \"3/3\",\n509\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"2/3\",\n510\t      \"cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range\": [\n511\t        1.7,\n512\t        3.1\n513\t      ]\n514\t    },\n515\t    \"tables\": [\n516\t      {\n517\t        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\",\n518\t        \"columns\": [\n519\t          \"step\",\n520\t          \"seed1_nve\",\n521\t          \"seed2_nve\",\n522\t          \"seed3_nve\",\n523\t          \"threshold\"\n524\t        ],\n525\t        \"rows\": [\n526\t          [\n527\t            25000,\n528\t            1.55,\n529\t            1.4,\n530\t            1.6,\n531\t            1.5\n532\t          ],\n533\t          [\n534\t            35000,\n535\t            1.85,\n536\t            1.7,\n537\t            1.9,\n538\t            1.5\n539\t          ],\n540\t          [\n541\t            45000,\n542\t            2.4,\n543\t            1.55,\n544\t            2.3,\n545\t            1.5\n546\t          ],\n547\t          [\n548\t            55000,\n549\t            3.1,\n550\t            1.2,\n551\t            2.8,\n552\t            1.5\n553\t          ],\n554\t          [\n555\t            65000,\n556\t            3.5,\n557\t            0.9,\n558\t            3.2,\n559\t            1.5\n560\t          ],\n561\t          [\n562\t            75000,\n563\t            null,\n564\t            0.7,\n565\t            null,\n566\t            1.5\n567\t          ],\n568\t          [\n569\t            105000,\n570\t            null,\n571\t            0.45,\n572\t            null,\n573\t            1.5\n574\t          ]\n575\t        ]\n576\t      },\n577\t      {\n578\t        \"name\": \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\",\n579\t        \"columns\": [\n580\t          \"environment\",\n581\t          \"seeds_above_1.5x\",\n582\t          \"collapse_rate\",\n583\t          \"nve_range_35K_plus\"\n584\t        ],\n585\t        \"rows\": [\n586\t          [\n587\t            \"Walker Walk\",\n588\t            \"2/2\",\n589\t            \"1/2\",\n590\t            \"[1.62, 4.12]\"\n591\t          ],\n592\t          [\n593\t            \"Cheetah Run\",\n594\t            \"3/3\",\n595\t            \"2/3\",\n596\t            \"[1.70, 3.10]\"\n597\t          ]\n598\t        ]\n599\t      }\n600\t    ],\n601\t    \"figures\": [],\n602\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE 1.5x in a second environment elevates the finding from \\\"Walker Walk specific\\\" to \\\"RSSM architectural vulnerability.\\\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5]. Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now has 5/5 separating power across environments for the \\\"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\\\" claim.\\n\\n\\nThe CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), not task-specific. The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates in Cheetah Run because the RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims). The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes: Cheetah Run's running dynamics involve rapid velocity changes that are poorly predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap that drives CIVO in Walker Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e calibration baseline is similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same systematic bias. Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah's dynamics may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\\n\"\n603\t  },\n604\t  {\n605\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n606\t    \"description\": \"Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no CIVO.\\n\",\n607\t    \"results\": {\n608\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 450,\n609\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.4,\n610\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 380,\n611\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n612\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 420,\n613\t      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n614\t      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 417,\n615\t      \"cross_seed_nve_range\": [\n616\t        0.35,\n617\t        0.6\n618\t      ],\n619\t      \"seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n620\t      \"collapse_count\": 0,\n621\t      \"reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline\": \"~70x\",\n622\t      \"cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus\": [\n623\t        0.35,\n624\t        0.6\n625\t      ]\n626\t    },\n627\t    \"tables\": [\n628\t      {\n629\t        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\",\n630\t        \"columns\": [\n631\t          \"step\",\n632\t          \"seed1_nve\",\n633\t          \"seed2_nve\",\n634\t          \"seed3_nve\"\n635\t        ],\n636\t        \"rows\": [\n637\t          [\n638\t            35000,\n639\t            0.8,\n640\t            0.85,\n641\t            0.78\n642\t          ],\n643\t          [\n644\t            55000,\n645\t            0.6,\n646\t            0.7,\n647\t            0.65\n648\t          ],\n649\t          [\n650\t            75000,\n651\t            0.45,\n652\t            0.58,\n653\t            0.5\n654\t          ],\n655\t          [\n656\t            105000,\n657\t            0.4,\n658\t            0.55,\n659\t            0.45\n660\t          ]\n661\t        ]\n662\t      }\n663\t    ],\n664\t    \"figures\": [],\n665\t    \"analysis\": \"Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete: 3/3 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect classification in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed, 0/5 untrained exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold accuracy = 1.0, well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\\n\\n\\nRemoving dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in Walker Walk: the critic cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward head loses dt-discriminative power (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays within normal range because the value function can only track actual return magnitudes, not inflated dt-specific estimates. The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50% of 850) due to reward prediction degradation (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off as Walker Walk's 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is within the tier-specified [0.3, 0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold's cross-env separating power.\\n\"\n666\t  },\n667\t  {\n668\t    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\",\n669\t    \"description\": \"V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x with near-baseline recovery.\\n\",\n670\t    \"results\": {\n671\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 710,\n672\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n673\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 680,\n674\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n675\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 740,\n676\t      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.52,\n677\t      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 710,\n678\t      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-16%\",\n679\t      \"nve_range\": [\n680\t        0.5,\n681\t        0.65\n682\t      ],\n683\t      \"v_recovery_pattern\": true,\n684\t      \"collapse_count\": 0\n685\t    },\n686\t    \"tables\": [],\n687\t    \"figures\": [],\n688\t    \"analysis\": \"Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection in both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x) and eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1 gradient → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\\n\\n\\nSame mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → no dt>1 in training data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains from dt=1 transitions only, providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic. NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes because it's driven by the exploration- exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic regime, which is architecture-dependent (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84% baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker Walk's 85% (778/916).\\n\"\n689\t  },\n690\t  {\n691\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\",\n692\t    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond locomotion to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\\n\",\n693\t    \"results\": {\n694\t      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 845,\n695\t      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 860,\n696\t      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 835,\n697\t      \"cross_seed_mean_peak\": 847,\n698\t      \"cross_seed_std\": 13,\n699\t      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.23,\n700\t      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.6,\n701\t      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~0.63-0.68\",\n702\t      \"convergence_step\": 100000\n703\t    },\n704\t    \"tables\": [],\n705\t    \"figures\": [],\n706\t    \"analysis\": \"Establishes clean reference. Cartpole's simpler observation space (5-dim) tests whether CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation — the key question is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified by trained dt_emb, are sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\\n\\n\\nDreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and 1-dim action make it a simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves good prediction quality rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed variance (~±13) because the task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence) provides the reference for NVE calculations.\\n\"\n707\t  },\n708\t  {\n709\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n710\t    \"description\": \"CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds.\\n\",\n711\t    \"results\": {\n712\t      \"seed1_nve_35K\": 1.65,\n713\t      \"seed1_nve_55K\": 2.2,\n714\t      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 150,\n715\t      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n716\t      \"seed2_nve_35K\": 1.55,\n717\t      \"seed2_nve_55K\": 1.4,\n718\t      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 380,\n719\t      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n720\t      \"seed3_nve_35K\": 1.75,\n721\t      \"seed3_nve_55K\": 2.5,\n722\t      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 120,\n723\t      \"seed3_collapse\": true,\n724\t      \"seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus\": \"3/3\",\n725\t      \"collapse_rate\": \"2/3\"\n726\t    },\n727\t    \"tables\": [\n728\t      {\n729\t        \"name\": \"Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\",\n730\t        \"columns\": [\n731\t          \"environment\",\n732\t          \"obs_dim\",\n733\t          \"seeds_above_1.5x\",\n734\t          \"collapse_rate\"\n735\t        ],\n736\t        \"rows\": [\n737\t          [\n738\t            \"Walker Walk\",\n739\t            24,\n740\t            \"2/2\",\n741\t            \"1/2\"\n742\t          ],\n743\t          [\n744\t            \"Cheetah Run\",\n745\t            17,\n746\t            \"3/3\",\n747\t            \"2/3\"\n748\t          ],\n749\t          [\n750\t            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n751\t            5,\n752\t            \"3/3\",\n753\t            \"2/3\"\n754\t          ]\n755\t        ]\n756\t      }\n757\t    ],\n758\t    \"figures\": [],\n759\t    \"analysis\": \"CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality. This is the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of RSSM + trained dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo = 3, matching the tier specification.\\n\\n\\nEven with Cartpole's simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through observation dimensionality per se. A 5-dim observation still has enough structure for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific representations that the RSSM can't support. The pole angle and angular velocity components are particularly sensitive to temporal skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the inverted position, so dt=2 predictions of these components degrade substantially. NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3 is within the tier-specified range.\\n\"\n760\t  },\n761\t  {\n762\t    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n763\t    \"description\": \"Causal control replication in third environment.\\n\",\n764\t    \"results\": {\n765\t      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.5,\n766\t      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n767\t      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n768\t      \"seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n769\t      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 480,\n770\t      \"collapse_count\": 0\n771\t    },\n772\t    \"tables\": [],\n773\t    \"figures\": [],\n774\t    \"analysis\": \"Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb → NVE<1.5x in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result.\\n\\n\\nSame causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding → no dt-conditioned value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result is expected to be robust because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation decoder gradient → systematic overestimation) is completely removed by setting dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment consistency of this control strengthens the causal claim from \\\"observed in one env\\\" to \\\"architecturally guaranteed.\\\"\\n\"\n775\t  },\n776\t  {\n777\t    \"name\": \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\",\n778\t    \"description\": \"Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results provide the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\\n\",\n779\t    \"results\": {\n780\t      \"baseline_obs_gap\": 2.04,\n781\t      \"baseline_reward_gap\": 1.56,\n782\t      \"008d_obs_gap\": 1.3,\n783\t      \"008d_obs_dt1_absolute\": 41.31,\n784\t      \"008d_obs_dt2_absolute\": 53.67,\n785\t      \"008d_reward_gap\": 2.78,\n786\t      \"008f_obs_gap\": 2.75,\n787\t      \"008f_reward_gap\": 1.01,\n788\t      \"008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline\": \"3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\"\n789\t    },\n790\t    \"tables\": [\n791\t      {\n792\t        \"name\": \"RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\",\n793\t        \"columns\": [\n794\t          \"model\",\n795\t          \"dt1_obs_MSE\",\n796\t          \"dt2_obs_MSE\",\n797\t          \"obs_gap\",\n798\t          \"dt1_reward_MAE\",\n799\t          \"dt2_reward_MAE\",\n800\t          \"reward_gap\"\n801\t        ],\n802\t        \"rows\": [\n803\t          [\n804\t            \"baseline\",\n805\t            13.49,\n806\t            27.5,\n807\t            \"2.04x\",\n808\t            0.0102,\n809\t            0.0159,\n810\t            \"1.56x\"\n811\t          ],\n812\t          [\n813\t            \"008d (trained)\",\n814\t            41.31,\n815\t            53.67,\n816\t            \"1.30x\",\n817\t            0.0568,\n818\t            0.1579,\n819\t            \"2.78x\"\n820\t          ],\n821\t          [\n822\t            \"008f (no dt_emb)\",\n823\t            8.01,\n824\t            22.04,\n825\t            \"2.75x\",\n826\t            0.7469,\n827\t            0.7526,\n828\t            \"1.01x\"\n829\t          ]\n830\t        ]\n831\t      }\n832\t    ],\n833\t    \"figures\": [],\n834\t    \"analysis\": \"Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d's 1.30x obs gap is misleading without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline). The reward gap (2.78x) directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific reward biases that the critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\\n\\n\\nThe RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions are systematically worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model (008d) shows paradoxically low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute error is already 3x baseline (model has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x) in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained dt_emb enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are systematically biased at dt=2 due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb), reward gap collapses to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing systematic bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\\n\"\n835\t  },\n836\t  {\n837\t    \"name\": \"Actor entropy analysis\",\n838\t    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO (Pathway A) from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\\n\",\n839\t    \"results\": {\n840\t      \"008d_s1_entropy_35K\": -3.47,\n841\t      \"008d_s1_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n842\t      \"008d_s1_entropy_direction\": \"decreasing (policy → deterministic)\",\n843\t      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K\": -5.21,\n844\t      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K\": -3.83,\n845\t      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction\": \"increasing (policy → random)\",\n846\t      \"008a_entropy_stable_range\": \"[-4.5, -3.8]\",\n847\t      \"008c_entropy_stable_range\": \"[-3.5, -2.8]\",\n848\t      \"baseline_entropy_converged\": -5.2\n849\t    },\n850\t    \"tables\": [\n851\t      {\n852\t        \"name\": \"Actor entropy at collapse/stability\",\n853\t        \"columns\": [\n854\t          \"config\",\n855\t          \"entropy_at_collapse_onset\",\n856\t          \"entropy_direction\",\n857\t          \"pathway\"\n858\t        ],\n859\t        \"rows\": [\n860\t          [\n861\t            \"008d s1 (CIVO)\",\n862\t            -3.47,\n863\t            \"↓ to -4.88\",\n864\t            \"A\"\n865\t          ],\n866\t          [\n867\t            \"008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\",\n868\t            -5.21,\n869\t            \"↑ to -3.83\",\n870\t            \"B\"\n871\t          ],\n872\t          [\n873\t            \"008a (healthy)\",\n874\t            -4.2,\n875\t            \"stable ±0.3\",\n876\t            \"-\"\n877\t          ],\n878\t          [\n879\t            \"baseline\",\n880\t            -5.0,\n881\t            \"stable ±0.2\",\n882\t            \"-\"\n883\t          ]\n884\t        ]\n885\t      }\n886\t    ],\n887\t    \"figures\": [],\n888\t    \"analysis\": \"This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides the most intuitive evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms, justifying the Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\\n\\n\\nActor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A and Pathway B. In classic CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides strong gradients toward a narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy becomes overconfident in the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy destabilizes from latent-space corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses confidence and explores randomly. Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable entropy in a moderate range, reflecting properly calibrated exploration-exploitation. The baseline converges to low entropy (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic policy, which is the correct behavior for Walker Walk.\\n\"\n889\t  },\n890\t  {\n891\t    \"name\": \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\",\n892\t    \"description\": \"1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level evidence for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\\n\",\n893\t    \"results\": {\n894\t      \"008d_35K_value_mean\": 60.2,\n895\t      \"008d_35K_return_mean\": 146.3,\n896\t      \"008d_35K_value_skewness\": 1.8,\n897\t      \"008d_55K_value_mean\": 137.9,\n898\t      \"008d_55K_return_mean\": 82.8,\n899\t      \"008d_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.85,\n900\t      \"008a_55K_value_mean\": 183.5,\n901\t      \"008a_55K_return_mean\": 487.9,\n902\t      \"008a_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.35,\n903\t      \"baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.4\n904\t    },\n905\t    \"tables\": [],\n906\t    \"figures\": [],\n907\t    \"analysis\": \"Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide a more nuanced view: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate all values — it specifically affects states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\\n\\n\\nThe scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where the divergence occurs. In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is right-skewed with 85% of value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic overestimation signature. In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution is symmetric around returns with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent with conservative critic). The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism: trained dt_emb creates a population of states where the critic is systematically optimistic, particularly for states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\\n\"\n908\t  },\n909\t  {\n910\t    \"name\": \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\",\n911\t    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings diverge during training.\\n\",\n912\t    \"results\": {\n913\t      \"008d_cosine_sim_5K\": 0.92,\n914\t      \"008d_cosine_sim_25K\": 0.65,\n915\t      \"008d_cosine_sim_35K\": 0.42,\n916\t      \"008d_cosine_sim_55K\": 0.28,\n917\t      \"008a_cosine_sim_55K\": 0.88,\n918\t      \"008f_cosine_sim\": \"N/A (no dt_emb)\",\n919\t      \"015b_cosine_sim\": 0.92,\n920\t      \"baseline_cosine_sim\": \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n921\t    },\n922\t    \"tables\": [\n923\t      {\n924\t        \"name\": \"dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\",\n925\t        \"columns\": [\n926\t          \"step\",\n927\t          \"008d (trained)\",\n928\t          \"008a (unexposed)\",\n929\t          \"015b (frozen)\"\n930\t        ],\n931\t        \"rows\": [\n932\t          [\n933\t            5000,\n934\t            0.92,\n935\t            0.92,\n936\t            0.92\n937\t          ],\n938\t          [\n939\t            15000,\n940\t            0.8,\n941\t            0.9,\n942\t            0.92\n943\t          ],\n944\t          [\n945\t            25000,\n946\t            0.65,\n947\t            0.89,\n948\t            0.92\n949\t          ],\n950\t          [\n951\t            35000,\n952\t            0.42,\n953\t            0.88,\n954\t            0.92\n955\t          ],\n956\t          [\n957\t            55000,\n958\t            0.28,\n959\t            0.88,\n960\t            0.92\n961\t          ]\n962\t        ]\n963\t      }\n964\t    ],\n965\t    \"figures\": [],\n966\t    \"analysis\": \"Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the vulnerability: gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions → CIVO. The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not causal proof by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and Full-SGS (018) evidence.\\n\\n\\nTrained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings (cosine ~0.92 from random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder gradient differentiates them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity has dropped to ~0.42 — the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling the critic to make highly dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample OFF, untrained on dt>1 data), cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the gradient signal only comes from dt=1 transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate. In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence timeline correlates with NVE onset, providing indirect evidence that embedding differentiation drives CIVO.\\n\"\n967\t  },\n968\t  {\n969\t    \"name\": \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\",\n970\t    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x threshold across all available data.\\n\",\n971\t    \"results\": {\n972\t      \"threshold_1.3x_accuracy\": 0.75,\n973\t      \"threshold_1.3x_false_positives\": 2,\n974\t      \"threshold_1.5x_accuracy\": 1.0,\n975\t      \"threshold_1.5x_false_positives\": 0,\n976\t      \"threshold_1.5x_false_negatives\": 0,\n977\t      \"threshold_1.7x_accuracy\": 0.875,\n978\t      \"threshold_1.7x_false_negatives\": 1,\n979\t      \"threshold_2.0x_accuracy\": 0.75,\n980\t      \"threshold_2.0x_false_negatives\": 2,\n981\t      \"auroc_walker_walk\": 1.0,\n982\t      \"optimal_threshold\": 1.5,\n983\t      \"cross_env_threshold_accuracy\": 1.0\n984\t    },\n985\t    \"tables\": [\n986\t      {\n987\t        \"name\": \"Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\",\n988\t        \"columns\": [\n989\t          \"threshold\",\n990\t          \"TP\",\n991\t          \"FP\",\n992\t          \"TN\",\n993\t          \"FN\",\n994\t          \"accuracy\",\n995\t          \"notes\"\n996\t        ],\n997\t        \"rows\": [\n998\t          [\n999\t            \"1.3x\",\n1000\t            2,\n1001\t            1,\n1002\t            1,\n1003\t            0,\n1004\t            \"3/4=0.75\",\n1005\t            \"009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\"\n1006\t          ],\n1007\t          [\n1008\t            \"1.5x\",\n1009\t            2,\n1010\t            0,\n1011\t            2,\n1012\t            0,\n1013\t            \"4/4=1.00\",\n1014\t            \"optimal\"\n1015\t          ],\n1016\t          [\n1017\t            \"1.7x\",\n1018\t            1,\n1019\t            0,\n1020\t            2,\n1021\t            1,\n1022\t            \"3/4=0.75\",\n1023\t            \"009b peak 1.62x missed\"\n1024\t          ],\n1025\t          [\n1026\t            \"2.0x\",\n1027\t            1,\n1028\t            0,\n1029\t            2,\n1030\t            1,\n1031\t            \"3/4=0.75\",\n1032\t            \"only sustained CIVO detected\"\n1033\t          ]\n1034\t        ]\n1035\t      },\n1036\t      {\n1037\t        \"name\": \"Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\",\n1038\t        \"columns\": [\n1039\t          \"environment\",\n1040\t          \"trained_above\",\n1041\t          \"untrained_below\",\n1042\t          \"accuracy\"\n1043\t        ],\n1044\t        \"rows\": [\n1045\t          [\n1046\t            \"Walker Walk\",\n1047\t            \"2/2\",\n1048\t            \"2/2\",\n1049\t            \"4/4\"\n1050\t          ],\n1051\t          [\n1052\t            \"Cheetah Run\",\n1053\t            \"3/3\",\n1054\t            \"3/3\",\n1055\t            \"6/6\"\n1056\t          ],\n1057\t          [\n1058\t            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n1059\t            \"3/3\",\n1060\t            \"3/3\",\n1061\t            \"6/6\"\n1062\t          ],\n1063\t          [\n1064\t            \"TOTAL\",\n1065\t            \"8/8\",\n1066\t            \"8/8\",\n1067\t            \"16/16\"\n1068\t          ]\n1069\t        ]\n1070\t      }\n1071\t    ],\n1072\t    \"figures\": [],\n1073\t    \"analysis\": \"1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances (including transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from natural v/e variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper's headline quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential — report it prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\\n\\n\\nAt 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification (2/2 trained above, 2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x, 008a somewhere near 1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted earlier. At 1.7x, 009b's peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative). At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold at 35K+ is the optimal operating point because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently by 35K (>0.24, making ratios reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e natural variation, and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current data. Cross-environment accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\\n\"\n1074\t  },\n1075\t  {\n1076\t    \"name\": \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\",\n1077\t    \"description\": \"Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty during imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\\n\",\n1078\t    \"results\": {\n1079\t      \"eval_105K\": 580,\n1080\t      \"nve_105K\": 0.95,\n1081\t      \"nve_peak\": 1.2,\n1082\t      \"collapse\": false,\n1083\t      \"truncation_rate_mean\": 0.35,\n1084\t      \"effective_horizon_mean\": 5.2,\n1085\t      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-37%\",\n1086\t      \"vs_008d_improvement\": true\n1087\t    },\n1088\t    \"tables\": [],\n1089\t    \"figures\": [],\n1090\t    \"analysis\": \"Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can be prevented by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality RSSM predictions → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval gap vs dt=1 imagination suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames future work direction.\\n\\n\\nUncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions degrade at dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the critic from ingesting low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement or learned variance as the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly 35% of imagination steps are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due to dt>1 predictions), reducing effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below 1.5x because the most overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However, eval (580) is lower than the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation reduces learning signal. This is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is targetable but the current implementation is not competitive with simply using dt=1 imagination.\\n\"\n1091\t  },\n1092\t  {\n1093\t    \"name\": \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\",\n1094\t    \"description\": \"Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\\n\",\n1095\t    \"results\": {\n1096\t      \"baseline_mean_peak\": 280,\n1097\t      \"baseline_ve_105K\": 0.58,\n1098\t      \"trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range\": [\n1099\t        1.5,\n1100\t        3.0\n1101\t      ],\n1102\t      \"trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x\": \"2/3\",\n1103\t      \"trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate\": \"1/3\",\n1104\t      \"no_dtEmb_nve_range\": [\n1105\t        0.4,\n1106\t        0.8\n1107\t      ],\n1108\t      \"no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n1109\t      \"cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env\": 0.92\n1110\t    },\n1111\t    \"tables\": [\n1112\t      {\n1113\t        \"name\": \"4-environment CIVO summary\",\n1114\t        \"columns\": [\n1115\t          \"environment\",\n1116\t          \"obs_dim\",\n1117\t          \"baseline_peak\",\n1118\t          \"trained_above_1.5x\",\n1119\t          \"untrained_above_1.5x\"\n1120\t        ],\n1121\t        \"rows\": [\n1122\t          [\n1123\t            \"Walker Walk\",\n1124\t            24,\n1125\t            950,\n1126\t            \"2/2\",\n1127\t            \"0/2\"\n1128\t          ],\n1129\t          [\n1130\t            \"Cheetah Run\",\n1131\t            17,\n1132\t            850,\n1133\t            \"3/3\",\n1134\t            \"0/3\"\n1135\t          ],\n1136\t          [\n1137\t            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n1138\t            5,\n1139\t            847,\n1140\t            \"3/3\",\n1141\t            \"0/3\"\n1142\t          ],\n1143\t          [\n1144\t            \"Hopper Hop\",\n1145\t            15,\n1146\t            280,\n1147\t            \"2/3\",\n1148\t            \"0/3\"\n1149\t          ]\n1150\t        ]\n1151\t      }\n1152\t    ],\n1153\t    \"figures\": [],\n1154\t    \"analysis\": \"P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed for CoRL submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak (280) reflects Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of absolute return magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one false negative — still within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\\n\\nHopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk and Cheetah Run — it requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with rewards primarily from forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 still applies because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging regardless of reward structure. The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs 3/3 in other envs) because Hopper's inherent instability means the baseline v/e ratio may be noisier, creating more variation in NVE denominators. Under best tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4 separating power per the tier specification. Combined with 3 other environments, cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\\n\"\n1155\t  }\n1156\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d55a81c1-4c5b-4fe3-9489-00d25d47d0f7","uuid":"667c0dfd-edfe-4ff4-a06d-4b4e34d937a6","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:02:15.777Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T133210/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb, collapse) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate CIVO collapse with 2 additional seeds. Registry already has data: 009e (seed 1) collapsed NVE peak 4.12x; 009b (seed 2) self-corrected NVE peak 2.05x. Under best tier, both seeds exceed NVE>1.5x (confirmed), collapse is stochastic 1/2 (confirmed).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 131,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak\": 4.12,\n      \"seed1_nve_peak_step\": 55000,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed1_collapse_step\": 55000,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 268,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak\": 2.05,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak_step\": 25000,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_self_correction_nve_105K\": 0.38,\n      \"both_seeds_exceed_1.5x\": true,\n      \"threshold_separating_power\": \"2/2 exceed 1.5x at 35K+\",\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"1/2 (stochastic)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"NVE trajectory comparison (seed 1 vs seed 2)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"seed1_nve\",\n          \"seed1_eval\",\n          \"seed2_nve\",\n          \"seed2_eval\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            25000,\n            1.78,\n            167,\n            2.05,\n            141\n          ],\n          [\n            35000,\n            1.71,\n            146,\n            1.24,\n            164\n          ],\n          [\n            45000,\n            2.09,\n            141,\n            1.62,\n            141\n          ],\n          [\n            55000,\n            4.12,\n            83,\n            1.41,\n            172\n          ],\n          [\n            65000,\n            1.23,\n            250,\n            0.94,\n            209\n          ],\n          [\n            75000,\n            1.48,\n            178,\n            0.66,\n            197\n          ],\n          [\n            85000,\n            1.15,\n            202,\n            0.51,\n            236\n          ],\n          [\n            95000,\n            1.73,\n            133,\n            0.43,\n            279\n          ],\n          [\n            105000,\n            1.57,\n            131,\n            0.38,\n            268\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Actual data confirms best-tier predictions. 2/2 trained dt_emb seeds exceed NVE 1.5x, maintaining perfect threshold separating power (now 4/4 across trained/untrained combined). Collapse stochasticity (1/2) is a feature of the narrative: trained dt_emb is a necessary condition for CIVO, not sufficient for collapse. The sustained-vs-transient NVE persistence distinguishes the two outcomes.\\n\\n\\nTrained dt_emb creates systematic overestimation because the RSSM fidelity gap at dt=2 (obs gap 1.30x at inflated absolute levels, reward gap 2.78x) is amplified through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient). Every seed with trained dt_emb reliably enters the pathological NVE>1.5x regime because the gradient mechanism is deterministic — the dt_emb learns to differentiate dt values, and the critic exploits this differentiation against inaccurate RSSM predictions. Whether NVE>1.5x becomes sustained (→collapse) or transient (→self-correction) depends on early training stochasticity: replay buffer sampling order, initialization, and whether the actor's policy gradient from real returns can counteract the critic's overestimate before it locks in (primacy bias, Qiao et al. 2023).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008f (dt_embed_dim=0, stable) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate causal control stability. Seed 1 (009f) was killed (infra). Seed 2 (009c) ran to 105K: NVE peaked 1.26x, eval declined to 148. Under best tier, seed 1 re-run shows NVE<1.5x; seed 2 extended to 200K plateaus without crossing 1.5x.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_rerun_eval_105K\": 420,\n      \"seed1_rerun_nve_peak\": 0.95,\n      \"seed1_rerun_nve_105K\": 0.35,\n      \"seed1_rerun_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 148,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 1.14,\n      \"seed2_eval_200K\": 280,\n      \"seed2_nve_200K\": 0.85,\n      \"seed2_nve_peak_ever\": 1.26,\n      \"seed2_crosses_1.5x\": false,\n      \"both_seeds_below_1.5x_at_35K_plus\": true,\n      \"threshold_separating_power\": \"0/2 exceed 1.5x\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Seed 2 extended trajectory (predicted 105K-200K)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"eval_return\",\n          \"nve\",\n          \"note\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            105000,\n            148,\n            1.14,\n            \"actual\"\n          ],\n          [\n            125000,\n            195,\n            1.05,\n            \"value still adjusting\"\n          ],\n          [\n            145000,\n            240,\n            0.92,\n            \"value correction begins\"\n          ],\n          [\n            165000,\n            270,\n            0.88,\n            \"eval recovering\"\n          ],\n          [\n            185000,\n            290,\n            0.85,\n            \"stabilizing\"\n          ],\n          [\n            200000,\n            280,\n            0.85,\n            \"plateau\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Seed 2's decline after 75K is concerning but does NOT constitute CIVO — NVE never exceeded 1.5x, and the value dynamics are qualitatively different (slow value tracking vs value explosion). The 200K extension confirms plateau without threshold crossing. Seed 1 re-run expected to show healthier zigzag recovery (like seed 0), improving cross-seed consistency. Critical for C2: 0/2 untrained seeds cross 1.5x, maintaining 4/4→6/6 threshold separation when combined with plan_011.\\n\\n\\nWithout dt_embedding (dim=0), the critic receives no dt-conditioning information. The reward head cannot distinguish dt=1 from dt=2 transitions (reward gap 1.01x vs 2.78x with trained dt_emb), so the systematic overestimation feedback loop cannot form. Seed 2's rising NVE at 105K (1.14x) reflects value inertia — value_mean was still rising (111.2@105K) while eval declined, but this is fundamentally different from CIVO: the value is simply slow to track a declining eval, not systematically overestimating. Extended to 200K, the value function adjusts downward as it accumulates more accurate return estimates, pulling NVE back below 1.0x. Seed 1 re-run (with stable infrastructure) follows the zigzag recovery pattern of 008f seed 0 (503@105K), because the underlying mechanism is the same — CIVO pathway is structurally blocked.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 (mitigation) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Multi-seed validation of dt=1 imagination mitigation. Already done: seed 1 success (869.9@105K), seed 2 collapsed (137@105K) via non-CIVO Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B gets clean mechanistic characterization.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 869.9,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.8,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 137,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 3.78,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed2_pathway\": \"B (eval-driven, entropy↑)\",\n      \"seed2_actor_entropy_collapse\": \"-5.21_to_-3.83\",\n      \"pathway_b_rate_walker\": \"1/2\",\n      \"pathway_a_blocked\": true,\n      \"gate_dt_mean_both_seeds\": 1.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Pathway A vs Pathway B signature comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"feature\",\n          \"pathway_a_008d_s1\",\n          \"pathway_b_008e_v3_s2\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"value change at collapse\",\n            \"+130% explosion\",\n            \"+13% mild\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"eval change at collapse\",\n            \"-43% follows value\",\n            \"-81% leads\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"NVE driver\",\n            \"value numerator\",\n            \"eval denominator\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"actor_entropy\",\n            \"↓ (-3.47→-4.88) deterministic\",\n            \"↑ (-5.21→-3.83) random\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"mechanism\",\n            \"critic overestimate → actor exploit\",\n            \"eval crash → value lag\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"C6 is partially falsified but transformed into a stronger finding: trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through MULTIPLE pathways. dt=1 imagination eliminates the more understood Pathway A but exposes the previously hidden Pathway B. Under best tier, Pathway B is characterized cleanly enough that it becomes a contribution rather than a weakness — the paper demonstrates that surface-level mitigations are insufficient.\\n\\n\\ndt=1 imagination blocks Pathway A (classic CIVO) because the critic never evaluates dt>1 imagination steps — the value overestimation feedback loop requires dt>1 predictions where RSSM fidelity degrades. However, the trained dt_embedding still receives gradient from observation decoders during world model training on real dt>1 transitions (subsample ON includes dt=2 in training data). This creates a secondary vulnerability: the dt_emb encodes dt-specific information that subtly corrupts the latent state representation, even when imagination uses only dt=1. Seed 2's collapse shows eval-driven destabilization (715→452→137) with increasing entropy (-5.21→-3.83), the opposite signature of CIVO (which has decreasing entropy). The policy destabilizes because the world model's latent space has been distorted by the trained dt_emb, not because the critic directly overestimates. This Pathway B is stochastic — seed 1 avoids it because its specific initialization leads to a latent representation that is less corrupted by the dt_emb gradients.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb, V-recovery) seeds 1-2\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate value growth suppression and V-recovery pattern with 2 additional seeds. Seed 0 showed 778@105K (85% baseline), NVE=0.59x with two dip-recovery cycles.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 720,\n      \"seed1_nve_range\": [\n        0.55,\n        0.9\n      ],\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.62,\n      \"seed1_v_recovery_count\": 2,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 690,\n      \"seed2_nve_range\": [\n        0.5,\n        0.95\n      ],\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.58,\n      \"seed2_v_recovery_count\": 1,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 729,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-20% (range -15% to -25%)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-seed V-recovery summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"seed\",\n          \"eval_55K\",\n          \"eval_65K\",\n          \"eval_85K\",\n          \"eval_105K\",\n          \"nve_55K\",\n          \"nve_105K\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            488,\n            608,\n            584,\n            778,\n            0.93,\n            0.59\n          ],\n          [\n            1,\n            450,\n            570,\n            620,\n            720,\n            0.85,\n            0.62\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            420,\n            540,\n            560,\n            690,\n            0.9,\n            0.58\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms C3 robustly: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection. The ±10% eval variance across seeds is well within DreamerV3's typical seed variability. The key invariant — NVE never exceeds 1.0x at 35K+ — holds across all seeds, consistent with the gradient-level explanation (dt_emb unexposed to dt>1 data → no dt>1-specific gradient signal → no systematic overestimation).\\n\\n\\nUntrained dt_emb (subsample OFF, so dt_emb trains but never sees dt>1 data) means the embedding learns from dt=1 transitions only. When imagination encounters dt>1 predictions, the dt_emb provides random-equivalent conditioning for those values. The critic cannot form dt-specific overestimates because the dt_emb carries no learned dt>1 information. NVE stays structurally suppressed (0.5-0.9x) because value growth is bounded by the quality of dt=1-only imagination. The V-recovery pattern (zigzag) arises from periodic exploration-exploitation oscillation in a regime where the critic is conservative. New seeds show similar pattern with ±10% variance in eval (driven by early exploration stochasticity), but the NVE suppression is robust because it's a structural property of the unexposed dt_emb, not a stochastic outcome.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk FROZEN dt_emb + subsample ON + dt=2 imag + H=8\",\n    \"description\": \"Already run as exp_015b. NVE 0.39-0.47 (far below 1.0), actor_loss→0 (gradient starvation), eval volatile 80-238. Under best tier, this confirms dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition for CIVO, with frozen dt_emb revealing a distinct failure mode (gradient starvation ≠ CIVO).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"eval_105K\": 204,\n      \"nve_range\": [\n        0.39,\n        0.47\n      ],\n      \"nve_105K\": 0.42,\n      \"actor_loss_105K\": 0.000179,\n      \"actor_entropy_105K\": -4.855,\n      \"collapse\": false,\n      \"civo_detected\": false,\n      \"gradient_starvation\": true,\n      \"gate_dt_mean\": 2.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"2×2 dt_emb ablation matrix (Walker Walk, H=8)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"config\",\n          \"dt_emb_status\",\n          \"nve_peak_35K_plus\",\n          \"collapse\",\n          \"failure_mode\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"008d\",\n            \"trained (grad ON)\",\n            \"4.12x\",\n            \"yes (1/2)\",\n            \"CIVO\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008f\",\n            \"absent (dim=0)\",\n            \"1.26x\",\n            \"no\",\n            \"reward degradation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"015b\",\n            \"frozen (grad OFF)\",\n            \"0.47x\",\n            \"no\",\n            \"gradient starvation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008c\",\n            \"untrained (subsample OFF)\",\n            \"0.90x\",\n            \"no\",\n            \"learning signal reduction\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"015b closes the \\\"is dt_emb TRAINING necessary?\\\" question definitively. The 2×2 matrix shows that only trained dt_emb with gradient flow produces NVE>1.5x. All other configurations avoid CIVO but have their own distinct degradation mechanisms — this is the nuanced picture that elevates the paper from \\\"trained dt_emb is bad\\\" to \\\"the gradient pathway through observation decoders→dt_emb is the specific vulnerability.\\\"\\n\\n\\nFrozen dt_emb (requires_grad=False, random init) provides constant random conditioning per dt value. The critic cannot learn dt-specific value adjustments → NVE stays far below 1.0x (0.39-0.47), confirming CIVO requires TRAINING of dt_emb. However, the frozen random embedding injects constant noise into the actor-critic system: the actor receives confusing value gradients (actor_loss→0 = gradient signal vanishes), and the gate locks to dt=2 100% because the gate network never receives informative gradients to adjust. This produces a distinct failure mode — not value overestimation (CIVO) but gradient starvation from persistent noise injection. The NVE being far below 1.0x (vs >1.5x for trained) is the clean separation: frozen dt_emb → NO CIVO pathway is possible. This completes the 2×2 ablation matrix: trained+present (008d)→CIVO, trained+absent (008f)→no CIVO, frozen+present (015b)→no CIVO but gradient starvation, confirming dt_emb TRAINING is the necessary condition.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Walker Walk 008e_v3 additional seeds (seed 3-4)\",\n    \"description\": \"Additional seeds to narrow Pathway B collapse probability. Current rate: 1/2 (seed 1 ok, seed 2 collapsed). Under best tier, Pathway B is rare (≤1/4 cross-env).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 840,\n      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.78,\n      \"seed3_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed4_eval_105K\": 810,\n      \"seed4_nve_105K\": 0.82,\n      \"seed4_collapse\": false,\n      \"pathway_b_rate_updated\": \"1/4 (25%)\",\n      \"pathway_b_rare_confirmed\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"008e_v3 cross-seed summary (4 seeds)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"seed\",\n          \"eval_105K\",\n          \"nve_105K\",\n          \"collapse\",\n          \"pathway\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            869.9,\n            0.8,\n            false,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            2,\n            137,\n            3.78,\n            true,\n            \"B (eval-driven)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            3,\n            840,\n            0.78,\n            false,\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            4,\n            810,\n            0.82,\n            false,\n            \"-\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"1/4 Pathway B rate is manageable for the narrative: \\\"dt=1 imagination eliminates the primary CIVO pathway in 4/4 seeds; a secondary eval-driven instability affects 1/4 seeds, characterized by increasing entropy rather than value explosion.\\\" This positions dt=1 imagination as a partial mitigation that eliminates the understood mechanism while leaving a residual vulnerability from the trained dt_emb's latent-space corruption.\\n\\n\\nUnder best tier, Pathway B is a rare event. With seeds 3-4 both succeeding, the rate drops from 1/2 to 1/4, consistent with the tier hypothesis that Pathway B is ≤1/4 frequency. The rationale: Pathway B requires a specific conjunction of early training conditions — the trained dt_emb must corrupt the latent representation in a way that destabilizes the actor without triggering the classic CIVO value explosion. This is a narrow failure mode that occurs when the dt_emb gradient from observation decoders creates a latent-space distortion that specifically disrupts the actor's policy gradient, which depends on seed-specific replay buffer composition. Most seeds avoid this because the distortion is mild enough that the actor adapts. The 1/4 rate makes Pathway B a secondary finding — acknowledged and characterized, but not the dominant failure mode (CIVO, Pathway A, remains primary).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in second DMC environment. Seed 1 (010a) already done: peak 849.8. Under best tier, 3 seeds establish clean reference at ~800-850.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 849.8,\n      \"seed1_eval_500K\": 849.8,\n      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 830,\n      \"seed2_eval_500K\": 815,\n      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 860,\n      \"seed3_eval_500K\": 840,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_peak\": 847,\n      \"cross_seed_std\": 15,\n      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.22,\n      \"ve_ratio_65K\": 0.5,\n      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.62,\n      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~0.65-0.70 from 135K+\",\n      \"convergence_step\": 120000\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run baseline v/e calibration\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"seed1_ve\",\n          \"seed2_ve\",\n          \"seed3_ve\",\n          \"mean_ve\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            25000,\n            0.09,\n            0.1,\n            0.08,\n            0.09\n          ],\n          [\n            35000,\n            0.22,\n            0.24,\n            0.21,\n            0.22\n          ],\n          [\n            55000,\n            0.38,\n            0.4,\n            0.37,\n            0.38\n          ],\n          [\n            75000,\n            0.55,\n            0.52,\n            0.56,\n            0.54\n          ],\n          [\n            105000,\n            0.62,\n            0.6,\n            0.64,\n            0.62\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Clean baseline establishes reference for cross-environment CIVO replication. The v/e calibration at ~0.22@35K provides the denominator for NVE threshold calculations in Cheetah Run experiments.\\n\\n\\nDreamerV3 is well-calibrated for Cheetah Run in the original paper. The RSSM handles Cheetah's 17-dim proprioceptive observation well. Baseline v/e calibration should be similar to Walker Walk (~0.65-0.70 at convergence) because the value function learns accurate return estimates when the world model is accurate (dt=1 only). The slightly lower peak than Walker Walk (850 vs 950) reflects Cheetah Run's harder reward landscape (requires coordinated leg movements for running gait). Seed variance ±15 is typical for DreamerV3 on DMC locomotion tasks.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Core cross-environment CIVO replication. exp_010b is RUNNING. Under best tier, NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ with collapse in 1-2/3 seeds.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 380,\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 95,\n      \"seed1_nve_35K\": 1.85,\n      \"seed1_nve_45K\": 2.4,\n      \"seed1_nve_55K\": 3.1,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed1_collapse_step\": 55000,\n      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 420,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 310,\n      \"seed2_nve_35K\": 1.7,\n      \"seed2_nve_45K\": 1.55,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed2_self_correction\": true,\n      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 350,\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 120,\n      \"seed3_nve_35K\": 1.9,\n      \"seed3_nve_55K\": 2.8,\n      \"seed3_collapse\": true,\n      \"cross_seed_nve_above_1.5x_count\": \"3/3\",\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"2/3\",\n      \"cheetah_civo_nve_35K_plus_range\": [\n        1.7,\n        3.1\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run CIVO NVE trajectories\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"seed1_nve\",\n          \"seed2_nve\",\n          \"seed3_nve\",\n          \"threshold\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            25000,\n            1.55,\n            1.4,\n            1.6,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            35000,\n            1.85,\n            1.7,\n            1.9,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            45000,\n            2.4,\n            1.55,\n            2.3,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            55000,\n            3.1,\n            1.2,\n            2.8,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            65000,\n            3.5,\n            0.9,\n            3.2,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            75000,\n            null,\n            0.7,\n            null,\n            1.5\n          ],\n          [\n            105000,\n            null,\n            0.45,\n            null,\n            1.5\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-environment CIVO comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"environment\",\n          \"seeds_above_1.5x\",\n          \"collapse_rate\",\n          \"nve_range_35K_plus\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Walker Walk\",\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"1/2\",\n            \"[1.62, 4.12]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cheetah Run\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"2/3\",\n            \"[1.70, 3.10]\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the critical experiment for the paper. 3/3 seeds exceeding NVE 1.5x in a second environment elevates the finding from \\\"Walker Walk specific\\\" to \\\"RSSM architectural vulnerability.\\\" The NVE range [1.70, 3.10] falls within the tier-specified [1.6, 3.5]. Combined with Walker Walk, the 1.5x threshold now has 5/5 separating power across environments for the \\\"trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x\\\" claim.\\n\\n\\nThe CIVO mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), not task-specific. The same gradient pathway (93% obs decoder→dt_emb) operates in Cheetah Run because the RSSM architecture is identical — only the observation dimensionality differs (17 vs 24 dims). The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 generalizes: Cheetah Run's running dynamics involve rapid velocity changes that are poorly predicted at dt=2 steps, creating the same obs_MSE gap that drives CIVO in Walker Walk. NVE 1.6-3.5x at 35K+ is expected because the v/e calibration baseline is similar (~0.22@35K), and the trained dt_emb introduces the same systematic bias. Collapse rate 2/3 (within best tier range [0.33, 0.67]) because Cheetah's dynamics may be slightly more chaotic, making the self-correction pathway harder to find.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Cross-environment causal control. Under best tier, NVE 0.3-0.9x, no CIVO.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 450,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.4,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 380,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 420,\n      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 417,\n      \"cross_seed_nve_range\": [\n        0.35,\n        0.6\n      ],\n      \"seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n      \"collapse_count\": 0,\n      \"reward_mae_ratio_vs_baseline\": \"~70x\",\n      \"cheetah_no_dt_emb_nve_35K_plus\": [\n        0.35,\n        0.6\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cheetah Run no-dt_emb causal control\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"seed1_nve\",\n          \"seed2_nve\",\n          \"seed3_nve\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            35000,\n            0.8,\n            0.85,\n            0.78\n          ],\n          [\n            55000,\n            0.6,\n            0.7,\n            0.65\n          ],\n          [\n            75000,\n            0.45,\n            0.58,\n            0.5\n          ],\n          [\n            105000,\n            0.4,\n            0.55,\n            0.45\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Combined with plan_021, the cross-environment causal control is complete: 3/3 trained dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x, 0/3 no-dt_emb seeds exceed 1.5x → 6/6 perfect classification in Cheetah Run. Across Walker Walk + Cheetah Run: 5/5 trained exceed, 0/5 untrained exceed → 10/10 perfect threshold classification. Cross-env threshold accuracy = 1.0, well within tier spec [0.83, 1.0].\\n\\n\\nRemoving dt_embedding in Cheetah Run has the same effect as in Walker Walk: the critic cannot exploit dt-conditioned predictions. The reward head loses dt-discriminative power (reward gap collapses to ~1.0x), and NVE stays within normal range because the value function can only track actual return magnitudes, not inflated dt-specific estimates. The absolute eval is lower than baseline (~50% of 850) due to reward prediction degradation (MAE inflated ~70x) — same trade-off as Walker Walk's 008f. NVE range [0.35, 0.60] is within the tier-specified [0.3, 0.9]. The 0/3 result maintains the threshold's cross-env separating power.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cheetah Run untrained dt_emb + no subsample (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"V-recovery pattern replication in Cheetah Run. Under best tier, NVE<1.0x with near-baseline recovery.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 710,\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.55,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 680,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 740,\n      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.52,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 710,\n      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-16%\",\n      \"nve_range\": [\n        0.5,\n        0.65\n      ],\n      \"v_recovery_pattern\": true,\n      \"collapse_count\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Confirms C3 cross-environment: untrained dt_emb provides structural protection in both Walker Walk and Cheetah Run, with consistent NVE suppression (0.5-0.6x) and eval recovery to ~85% baseline. The mechanism (unexposed dt_emb → no dt>1 gradient → no systematic overestimation) is environment-agnostic.\\n\\n\\nSame mechanism as Walker Walk 008a: untrained dt_emb (subsample OFF → no dt>1 in training data) blocks the CIVO gradient pathway. The dt_emb trains from dt=1 transitions only, providing no dt>1-specific information to the critic. NVE is structurally suppressed below 1.0x. The V-recovery pattern generalizes because it's driven by the exploration- exploitation oscillation in a conservative-critic regime, which is architecture-dependent (RSSM), not task-dependent. Eval at ~84% baseline (710/850) is consistent with Walker Walk's 85% (778/916).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"dt=1 baseline in non-locomotion DMC environment. Broadens scope beyond locomotion to test whether CIVO depends on movement dynamics.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_eval_peak\": 845,\n      \"seed2_eval_peak\": 860,\n      \"seed3_eval_peak\": 835,\n      \"cross_seed_mean_peak\": 847,\n      \"cross_seed_std\": 13,\n      \"ve_ratio_35K\": 0.23,\n      \"ve_ratio_105K\": 0.6,\n      \"ve_ratio_stable\": \"~0.63-0.68\",\n      \"convergence_step\": 100000\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Establishes clean reference. Cartpole's simpler observation space (5-dim) tests whether CIVO still occurs when the RSSM has less room for fidelity degradation — the key question is whether even small absolute errors in dt>1 prediction, amplified by trained dt_emb, are sufficient to trigger NVE>1.5x.\\n\\n\\nDreamerV3 handles Cartpole Swingup well — 5-dim observation and 1-dim action make it a simpler learning problem than locomotion. The RSSM achieves good prediction quality rapidly. Baseline peaks at ~840-860 with tight cross-seed variance (~±13) because the task has lower stochasticity than locomotion. The v/e calibration (~0.23@35K, ~0.63@convergence) provides the reference for NVE calculations.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"CIVO trigger in non-locomotion environment. Under best tier, NVE>1.5x in ≥2/3 seeds.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_nve_35K\": 1.65,\n      \"seed1_nve_55K\": 2.2,\n      \"seed1_eval_105K\": 150,\n      \"seed1_collapse\": true,\n      \"seed2_nve_35K\": 1.55,\n      \"seed2_nve_55K\": 1.4,\n      \"seed2_eval_105K\": 380,\n      \"seed2_collapse\": false,\n      \"seed3_nve_35K\": 1.75,\n      \"seed3_nve_55K\": 2.5,\n      \"seed3_eval_105K\": 120,\n      \"seed3_collapse\": true,\n      \"seeds_above_1.5x_35K_plus\": \"3/3\",\n      \"collapse_rate\": \"2/3\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-environment CIVO summary (3 envs)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"environment\",\n          \"obs_dim\",\n          \"seeds_above_1.5x\",\n          \"collapse_rate\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Walker Walk\",\n            24,\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"1/2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cheetah Run\",\n            17,\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"2/3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n            5,\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"2/3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"CIVO triggers in 3/3 environments regardless of observation dimensionality. This is the strongest evidence that CIVO is an architectural vulnerability of RSSM + trained dt_emb, not a task-specific phenomenon. environments_with_confirmed_civo = 3, matching the tier specification.\\n\\n\\nEven with Cartpole's simpler 5-dim observation, the RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 is sufficient to trigger CIVO. The mechanism operates through the observation decoder→dt_emb gradient pathway (93% of dt_emb gradient), not through observation dimensionality per se. A 5-dim observation still has enough structure for the dt_emb to learn dt-specific representations that the RSSM can't support. The pole angle and angular velocity components are particularly sensitive to temporal skipping — Cartpole dynamics are chaotic near the inverted position, so dt=2 predictions of these components degrade substantially. NVE>1.5x in 3/3 seeds maintains the perfect threshold separating power. Collapse in 2/3 is within the tier-specified range.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cartpole Swingup dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON (3 seeds)\",\n    \"description\": \"Causal control replication in third environment.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"seed1_nve_105K\": 0.5,\n      \"seed2_nve_105K\": 0.6,\n      \"seed3_nve_105K\": 0.45,\n      \"seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n      \"cross_seed_mean_eval_105K\": 480,\n      \"collapse_count\": 0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Across 3 environments: trained dt_emb → NVE>1.5x in 8/8 seeds; no dt_emb → NVE<1.5x in 0/8 seeds. Threshold classification accuracy = 16/16 = 1.0. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result.\\n\\n\\nSame causal mechanism as Walker Walk and Cheetah Run: no dt_embedding → no dt-conditioned value exploitation → NVE stays within normal range. The result is expected to be robust because the causal pathway (trained dt_emb → observation decoder gradient → systematic overestimation) is completely removed by setting dt_embed_dim=0. Cross-environment consistency of this control strengthens the causal claim from \\\"observed in one env\\\" to \\\"architecturally guaranteed.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"RSSM prediction quality: dt=1 vs dt=2 transitions\",\n    \"description\": \"Already completed (exp_rssm_fidelity_analysis). Under best tier, results provide the mechanistic foundation showing RSSM fidelity gap drives CIVO.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"baseline_obs_gap\": 2.04,\n      \"baseline_reward_gap\": 1.56,\n      \"008d_obs_gap\": 1.3,\n      \"008d_obs_dt1_absolute\": 41.31,\n      \"008d_obs_dt2_absolute\": 53.67,\n      \"008d_reward_gap\": 2.78,\n      \"008f_obs_gap\": 2.75,\n      \"008f_reward_gap\": 1.01,\n      \"008d_dt1_obs_vs_baseline\": \"3.06x (41.31 / 13.49)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"RSSM Fidelity (absolute + ratio)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"model\",\n          \"dt1_obs_MSE\",\n          \"dt2_obs_MSE\",\n          \"obs_gap\",\n          \"dt1_reward_MAE\",\n          \"dt2_reward_MAE\",\n          \"reward_gap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"baseline\",\n            13.49,\n            27.5,\n            \"2.04x\",\n            0.0102,\n            0.0159,\n            \"1.56x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008d (trained)\",\n            41.31,\n            53.67,\n            \"1.30x\",\n            0.0568,\n            0.1579,\n            \"2.78x\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008f (no dt_emb)\",\n            8.01,\n            22.04,\n            \"2.75x\",\n            0.7469,\n            0.7526,\n            \"1.01x\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Must present absolute values AND ratios side-by-side. 008d's 1.30x obs gap is misleading without the absolute context (41.31 at dt=1 = 3x baseline). The reward gap (2.78x) directly explains CIVO: trained dt_emb creates dt-specific reward biases that the critic amplifies through the imagination rollout.\\n\\n\\nThe RSSM fidelity analysis reveals the root cause: dt=2 predictions are systematically worse than dt=1 across all models. The trained dt_emb model (008d) shows paradoxically low obs_gap ratio (1.30x) because its dt=1 absolute error is already 3x baseline (model has degraded overall). The reward gap (2.78x) in 008d is the smoking gun — the trained dt_emb enables the reward head to make dt-specific predictions, but these predictions are systematically biased at dt=2 due to the underlying RSSM fidelity gap. In 008f (no dt_emb), reward gap collapses to 1.01x — the reward head treats dt=1 and dt=2 identically, preventing systematic bias. This explains why 008f avoids CIVO despite higher absolute reward MAE.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Actor entropy analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — extract from existing metrics.jsonl logs. Separates CIVO (Pathway A) from non-CIVO (Pathway B) collapse signatures.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"008d_s1_entropy_35K\": -3.47,\n      \"008d_s1_entropy_55K\": -4.88,\n      \"008d_s1_entropy_direction\": \"decreasing (policy → deterministic)\",\n      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_85K\": -5.21,\n      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_105K\": -3.83,\n      \"008e_v3_s2_entropy_direction\": \"increasing (policy → random)\",\n      \"008a_entropy_stable_range\": \"[-4.5, -3.8]\",\n      \"008c_entropy_stable_range\": \"[-3.5, -2.8]\",\n      \"baseline_entropy_converged\": -5.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Actor entropy at collapse/stability\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"config\",\n          \"entropy_at_collapse_onset\",\n          \"entropy_direction\",\n          \"pathway\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"008d s1 (CIVO)\",\n            -3.47,\n            \"↓ to -4.88\",\n            \"A\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008e_v3 s2 (non-CIVO)\",\n            -5.21,\n            \"↑ to -3.83\",\n            \"B\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"008a (healthy)\",\n            -4.2,\n            \"stable ±0.3\",\n            \"-\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"baseline\",\n            -5.0,\n            \"stable ±0.2\",\n            \"-\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This figure is essential for the dual-pathway narrative. It provides the most intuitive evidence that 008d and 008e_v3_s2 fail through different mechanisms, justifying the Pathway A / Pathway B framing.\\n\\n\\nActor entropy is the cleanest discriminator between Pathway A and Pathway B. In classic CIVO (Pathway A, 008d), the overestimating critic provides strong gradients toward a narrow set of actions → entropy decreases as the policy becomes overconfident in the wrong actions. In Pathway B (008e_v3_s2), the policy destabilizes from latent-space corruption → entropy increases as the actor loses confidence and explores randomly. Healthy configs (008a, 008c) maintain stable entropy in a moderate range, reflecting properly calibrated exploration-exploitation. The baseline converges to low entropy (~-5.2) reflecting a well-learned deterministic policy, which is the correct behavior for Walker Walk.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Value prediction distribution vs realized returns\",\n    \"description\": \"1 GPU hour — load checkpoints and evaluate. Shows distribution-level evidence for CIVO beyond the scalar v/e ratio.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"008d_35K_value_mean\": 60.2,\n      \"008d_35K_return_mean\": 146.3,\n      \"008d_35K_value_skewness\": 1.8,\n      \"008d_55K_value_mean\": 137.9,\n      \"008d_55K_return_mean\": 82.8,\n      \"008d_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.85,\n      \"008a_55K_value_mean\": 183.5,\n      \"008a_55K_return_mean\": 487.9,\n      \"008a_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.35,\n      \"baseline_55K_value_above_return_frac\": 0.4\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Addresses R3 (v/e is crude proxy) directly. The distribution plots provide a more nuanced view: CIVO doesn't uniformly inflate all values — it specifically affects states reachable via dt>1 imagination, creating a bimodal distribution.\\n\\n\\nThe scalar v/e ratio is a proxy; distribution plots show where the divergence occurs. In 008d at 55K (pre-collapse), the value distribution is right-skewed with 85% of value predictions exceeding realized returns — a systematic overestimation signature. In 008a (healthy, untrained dt_emb), the value distribution is symmetric around returns with only 35% exceeding (slight underestimation, consistent with conservative critic). The distribution shape directly shows the CIVO mechanism: trained dt_emb creates a population of states where the critic is systematically optimistic, particularly for states that would be reached via dt>1 imagination.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dt_embedding cosine similarity analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — extract dt_emb weights from checkpoints. Shows how dt=1 and dt=2 embeddings diverge during training.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"008d_cosine_sim_5K\": 0.92,\n      \"008d_cosine_sim_25K\": 0.65,\n      \"008d_cosine_sim_35K\": 0.42,\n      \"008d_cosine_sim_55K\": 0.28,\n      \"008a_cosine_sim_55K\": 0.88,\n      \"008f_cosine_sim\": \"N/A (no dt_emb)\",\n      \"015b_cosine_sim\": 0.92,\n      \"baseline_cosine_sim\": \"N/A (no dt_emb)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"dt_emb cosine similarity evolution\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"step\",\n          \"008d (trained)\",\n          \"008a (unexposed)\",\n          \"015b (frozen)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            5000,\n            0.92,\n            0.92,\n            0.92\n          ],\n          [\n            15000,\n            0.8,\n            0.9,\n            0.92\n          ],\n          [\n            25000,\n            0.65,\n            0.89,\n            0.92\n          ],\n          [\n            35000,\n            0.42,\n            0.88,\n            0.92\n          ],\n          [\n            55000,\n            0.28,\n            0.88,\n            0.92\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Provides a mechanistic visualization of WHY trained dt_emb creates the vulnerability: gradient-driven embedding divergence → dt-specific critic predictions → CIVO. The correlation between divergence and NVE onset is compelling but not causal proof by itself — it complements the gradient decomposition (D061) and Full-SGS (018) evidence.\\n\\n\\nTrained dt_emb (008d) starts with similar dt=1/dt=2 embeddings (cosine ~0.92 from random init) and diverges rapidly as the observation decoder gradient differentiates them. By 35K (when NVE first exceeds 1.5x), cosine similarity has dropped to ~0.42 — the embeddings are now substantially different, enabling the critic to make highly dt-specific (and wrong) predictions. In 008a (subsample OFF, untrained on dt>1 data), cosine similarity stays high (~0.88) because the gradient signal only comes from dt=1 transitions, providing no pressure to differentiate. In 015b (frozen), cosine similarity stays at initialization (~0.92). The divergence timeline correlates with NVE onset, providing indirect evidence that embedding differentiation drives CIVO.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"NVE threshold sensitivity analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"0 GPU cost — computed from existing metrics. Tests robustness of 1.5x threshold across all available data.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"threshold_1.3x_accuracy\": 0.75,\n      \"threshold_1.3x_false_positives\": 2,\n      \"threshold_1.5x_accuracy\": 1.0,\n      \"threshold_1.5x_false_positives\": 0,\n      \"threshold_1.5x_false_negatives\": 0,\n      \"threshold_1.7x_accuracy\": 0.875,\n      \"threshold_1.7x_false_negatives\": 1,\n      \"threshold_2.0x_accuracy\": 0.75,\n      \"threshold_2.0x_false_negatives\": 2,\n      \"auroc_walker_walk\": 1.0,\n      \"optimal_threshold\": 1.5,\n      \"cross_env_threshold_accuracy\": 1.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Threshold sensitivity (Walker Walk, 35K+ window)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"threshold\",\n          \"TP\",\n          \"FP\",\n          \"TN\",\n          \"FN\",\n          \"accuracy\",\n          \"notes\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1.3x\",\n            2,\n            1,\n            1,\n            0,\n            \"3/4=0.75\",\n            \"009c@105K=1.14x → FP edge case\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.5x\",\n            2,\n            0,\n            2,\n            0,\n            \"4/4=1.00\",\n            \"optimal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.7x\",\n            1,\n            0,\n            2,\n            1,\n            \"3/4=0.75\",\n            \"009b peak 1.62x missed\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2.0x\",\n            1,\n            0,\n            2,\n            1,\n            \"3/4=0.75\",\n            \"only sustained CIVO detected\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-environment threshold accuracy (best tier, 1.5x)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"environment\",\n          \"trained_above\",\n          \"untrained_below\",\n          \"accuracy\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Walker Walk\",\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"4/4\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cheetah Run\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"6/6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"6/6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"TOTAL\",\n            \"8/8\",\n            \"8/8\",\n            \"16/16\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"1.5x is the Goldilocks threshold: tight enough to catch all CIVO instances (including transient ones like 009b), loose enough to avoid false positives from natural v/e variation. AUROC=1.0 across environments under best tier is the paper's headline quantitative result for C2. The 35K+ time window constraint is essential — report it prominently to prevent misapplication at earlier steps.\\n\\n\\nAt 1.5x, the current Walker Walk data has 4/4 perfect classification (2/2 trained above, 2/2 untrained below). At 1.3x, two untrained seeds (009c@105K=1.14x, 008a somewhere near 1.0x) could create false positives if the window is shifted earlier. At 1.7x, 009b's peak of 1.62x@45K would be missed (false negative). At 2.0x, both 009b (2.05x@25K but this is pre-35K) and potentially 008d at 35K (1.71x) would be missed. The 1.5x threshold at 35K+ is the optimal operating point because: (a) baseline v/e has converged sufficiently by 35K (>0.24, making ratios reliable), (b) 1.5x is approximately 2σ above baseline v/e natural variation, and (c) it achieves perfect sensitivity and specificity in the current data. Cross-environment accuracy stays at 1.0 under best tier because the mechanism is architecture-specific (RSSM + trained dt_emb), so the threshold generalizes.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Pilot for principled CIVO prevention: monitor RSSM prediction uncertainty during imagination rollout, truncate when uncertainty exceeds threshold.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"eval_105K\": 580,\n      \"nve_105K\": 0.95,\n      \"nve_peak\": 1.2,\n      \"collapse\": false,\n      \"truncation_rate_mean\": 0.35,\n      \"effective_horizon_mean\": 5.2,\n      \"vs_baseline_105K\": \"-37%\",\n      \"vs_008d_improvement\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Primarily a discussion section contribution: demonstrates that CIVO can be prevented by uncertainty-aware truncation, confirming the causal chain (low-quality RSSM predictions → value overestimation). Not a practical solution yet — the eval gap vs dt=1 imagination suggests room for smarter truncation policies. Frames future work direction.\\n\\n\\nUncertainty-gated truncation addresses the root cause: RSSM predictions degrade at dt>1, so truncating imagination when uncertainty rises prevents the critic from ingesting low-quality predictions. The pilot uses ensemble disagreement or learned variance as the uncertainty signal. At dt=2 with subsample ON, roughly 35% of imagination steps are truncated (those where RSSM uncertainty spikes due to dt>1 predictions), reducing effective horizon from H=8 to ~5.2. NVE stays below 1.5x because the most overestimation-prone imagination steps are removed. However, eval (580) is lower than the dt=1 imagination baseline (870) because truncation reduces learning signal. This is a proof-of-concept — it shows the mechanism is targetable but the current implementation is not competitive with simply using dt=1 imagination.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Hopper Hop validation (3 seeds × 3 configs)\",\n    \"description\": \"Third DMC locomotion environment. Under best tier, confirms CIVO generalization with different reward structure (sparse contact rewards).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"baseline_mean_peak\": 280,\n      \"baseline_ve_105K\": 0.58,\n      \"trained_dtEmb_nve_35K_range\": [\n        1.5,\n        3.0\n      ],\n      \"trained_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x\": \"2/3\",\n      \"trained_dtEmb_collapse_rate\": \"1/3\",\n      \"no_dtEmb_nve_range\": [\n        0.4,\n        0.8\n      ],\n      \"no_dtEmb_seeds_above_1.5x\": \"0/3\",\n      \"cross_env_threshold_accuracy_4env\": 0.92\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"4-environment CIVO summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"environment\",\n          \"obs_dim\",\n          \"baseline_peak\",\n          \"trained_above_1.5x\",\n          \"untrained_above_1.5x\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Walker Walk\",\n            24,\n            950,\n            \"2/2\",\n            \"0/2\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cheetah Run\",\n            17,\n            850,\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"0/3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cartpole Swingup\",\n            5,\n            847,\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"0/3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Hopper Hop\",\n            15,\n            280,\n            \"2/3\",\n            \"0/3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"P2 priority — strengthens the generalization claim but not strictly needed for CoRL submission (3 environments already sufficient). The lower baseline peak (280) reflects Hopper Hop's difficulty, but the NVE mechanism is independent of absolute return magnitude. If 2/3 instead of 3/3 exceed 1.5x, this creates one false negative — still within the tier-specified ≥3/4 cross-env separating power.\\n\\nHopper Hop has a different reward structure from Walker Walk and Cheetah Run — it requires hopping (leaving ground contact repeatedly), with rewards primarily from forward velocity during airborne phases. The RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1 still applies because multi-step dynamics prediction is challenging regardless of reward structure. The NVE>1.5x rate may be slightly lower (2/3 vs 3/3 in other envs) because Hopper's inherent instability means the baseline v/e ratio may be noisier, creating more variation in NVE denominators. Under best tier, the threshold still achieves ≥3/4 separating power per the tier specification. Combined with 3 other environments, cross-env accuracy stays high (0.92+).\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1156,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1156}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BVzuYB2PvceZCnjTM4TF1T","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n2\t\n3\t## Title Candidates\n4\t\n5\t1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n6\t2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n7\t3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n8\t4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n9\t\n10\t**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n11\t\n12\t**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n13\t\n14\t## Venue\n15\t\n16\t**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n17\t\n18\t## Narrative Positioning\n19\t\n20\tThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n21\t\n22\tThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n23\t\n24\tWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## Core Claims\n29\t\n30\t### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n31\t\n32\tTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n33\t\n34\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n35\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n36\t| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n37\t| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n38\t| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n39\t| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n40\t\n41\t**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n42\t\n43\t### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n44\t\n45\t**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n46\t\n47\tThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n48\t\n49\t- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n50\t- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n51\t- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n52\t\n53\t**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n54\t\n55\t**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n56\t\n57\tThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n58\t\n59\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n60\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n61\t| Untrained dt_emb, no collapse | exp_008c (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval plateau ~242@55K; v/e: @35K=21.8% (0.90x) → @55K=29.1% (0.72x) → @65K=32.8% (0.58x) (declining normalized v/e, ~7x slower than trained) |\n62\t| Trained dt_emb, collapse | exp_008d (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval declining 167→146→141 @25K→35K→45K; v/e: @25K=15.5% (1.78x) → @35K=41.2% (1.71x) → @45K=69.3% (2.09x) (accelerating overestimation) |\n63\t| Both trained dt_emb → accelerating v/e | exp_006 (H=15) vs exp_008d (H=8) | 006: v/e @35K=31.1% (1.29x)→@45K=53.3% (1.61x)→@55K=93.3% (2.31x); 008d: v/e @35K=41.2% (1.71x)→@45K=69.3% (2.09x) — both show accelerating normalized v/e leading to collapse |\n64\t| Baseline v/e calibration (overshoot reference) | exp_001_baseline | v/e@35K=24.1%, @45K=33.1%, @55K=40.4%. Overshoot ratio: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x (all >1.5x = pathological) |\n65\t| **D032: Trained dt_emb seed 2 — pathological but self-correcting** | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 39→141→164→141→172→209→197→236→279→268@105K (**no collapse**, rising). nve: **2.05x@25K**→1.24x→**1.62x@45K**→1.41x→0.94x→0.66x→0.51x→0.43x→**0.38x@105K**. Transient nve>1.5x with self-correction. Value peaked 110.9@65K → self-corrected to 67.3@105K while eval improved. |\n66\t| **1.5x threshold separating power** | 008d+009b vs 008f+009c | WITH trained dt_emb: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008d peak 2.09x sustained, 009b peak 2.05x transient). WITHOUT dt_emb: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008f peak 1.02x, 009c peak 1.26x). **4/4 perfect classification.** |\n67\t| **Causal control: dt_embed_dim=0 (direction confirmed, seed-dependent)** | exp_008f seed 1 + exp_009c seed 2 | **Seed 1**: eval 197→207→194→173→503@105K (zigzag recovery); nve 1.02x→0.73x→0.83x→0.97x→0.30x. **Seed 2**: eval 169→211→239→359→227→191→148@105K (decline after 75K); nve 1.26x→0.79x→0.72x→0.33x→0.60x→0.90x→1.14x. Both vs 008d@45K nve=2.09x: onset delayed 60K+ steps, severity reduced ~2x. **009c extending to 200K to observe if nve crosses 1.5x.** |\n68\t| **Reward SGS = no-op (reward gradient = 0 by architecture)** | exp_016e (reward_dt_emb_detach=True, subsample ON, H=8, seed 1) | Peak eval 326.7@75K → collapse 31.7@95K, NVE=10.42x. 梯度验证揭示 reward loss 对 dt_emb.weight 贡献为 0——reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异。**dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders（orientations/velocity/height），7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。** |\n69\t| RSSM fidelity analysis | baseline / 008d / 008f | See RSSM Fidelity Table below |\n70\t\n71\t**⚠️ 008f 定位：因果控制实验，非推荐方案。** 008f 去掉 dt_embedding 后 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 的 75x），reward head 无法区分 dt=1/dt=2（reward gap 仅 1.01x vs 008d 的 2.78x）。**D030 更新**：008f seed 2 (009c) 在 105K 达到 nve=1.14x（borderline），seed 1 为 0.30x——protection 不 robust。但 008f 两个 seed 的 v/e 增长速度均显著慢于 008d（008d@45K=2.09x vs 008f seed 2@105K=1.14x），确认 dt_embedding 是 primary amplifier。**C2 因果方向成立，但 \"eliminates\" 改为 \"significantly delays and reduces\"。推荐方案仍是 008e_v3（C6）**。\n72\t\n73\t**RSSM Fidelity Table（论文中必须同时报告绝对误差和比率）：**\n74\t\n75\t| Model | dt1 obs_MSE | dt2 obs_MSE | obs gap | dt1 reward_MAE | dt2 reward_MAE | reward gap |\n76\t|-------|------------|------------|---------|---------------|---------------|------------|\n77\t| baseline (exp_001) | 13.49 | 27.50 | **2.04x** | 0.0102 | 0.0159 | 1.56x |\n78\t| 008d (trained dt_emb, collapsed) | 41.31 | 53.67 | **1.30x** | 0.0568 | 0.1579 | **2.78x** |\n79\t| 008f (no dt_emb, stable) | 8.01 | 22.04 | **2.75x** | 0.7469 | 0.7526 | 1.01x |\n80\t\n81\t**解读要点**：008d 的低 obs gap ratio (1.30x) 具有误导性——其 dt=1 绝对误差本身已是 baseline 的 3x（模型已崩塌）。论文中必须并排呈现绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被比率误导。\n82\t\n83\t**dt_emb Gradient Decomposition (D061)**\n84\t\n85\t梯度验证（torch.autograd.grad analysis, commit 5fc8e49）揭示 dt_emb.weight 的梯度来源分布：\n86\t\n87\t| Source | Grad Norm | Fraction |\n88\t|--------|-----------|----------|\n89\t| Observation decoders (orientations, velocity, height) | 1.505 | 93% |\n90\t| KL dynamics | 0.119 | 7% |\n91\t| Cont head | ~0 (included in decoder total) | <1% |\n92\t| **Reward head** | **0.000** | **0%** |\n93\t| **Total** | **1.624** | **100%** |\n94\t\n95\t这意味着：(1) 016e 的 reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op——detach 了一个不存在的梯度通路；(2) dt_emb 的训练几乎完全由 observation reconstruction loss 驱动；(3) Full-SGS（exp_018）移除 observation decoder 梯度后仅保留 7% KL 梯度，是真正的 gradient-level intervention。\n96\t\n97\t**Strength**: Moderate-strong (D032 update: causal direction + diagnostic marker confirmed; collapse stochastic). The **nve>1.5x threshold** achieves 4/4 perfect separation: all trained dt_emb seeds (008d, 009b) exceed 1.5x; no untrained seeds (008f, 009c) exceed 1.5x. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2. **Collapse** is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds): 008d seed 1 shows sustained nve>1.5x → collapse; 009b seed 2 shows transient nve>1.5x → self-correction. The **causal direction** is unambiguous across all 4 data points. Pending: 009e (008d seed 1) to determine collapse probability (1/3 vs 2/3); 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c extension to 200K.\n98\t\n99\t### C3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, substantively protecting performance\n100\t\n101\tWhen dt_embedding is untrained (random fixed), the value function cannot exploit precise dt information, preventing the optimistic value drift seen in trained variants. This value growth suppression substantively protects performance: exp_008a demonstrates two complete zigzag recovery cycles over 105K steps (peak @105K=778, 85% of baseline 916), with normalized v/e consistently suppressed at 0.59-0.93x baseline. The trained dt_embedding variant (exp_006) collapses catastrophically as its v/e ratio accelerates far past baseline levels (2.3x baseline@55K).\n102\t\n103\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n104\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n105\t| Two V-shaped recoveries (105K) | exp_008a (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | 501→563→488→608→470→584→572→778; two complete recovery cycles over 105K steps. @65K=608 (-1.3% baseline), @105K=778 (-15.1% baseline, 85% of 916). No collapse. |\n106\t| Sustained v/e suppression | exp_008a | v/e: 37.6%@55K → 37.2%@65K → 53.6%@75K → 47.5%@85K → 49.7%@95K → 38.6%@105K. Normalized v/e: 0.93x→0.66x→0.75x→0.68x→0.79x→0.59x baseline. Consistently below 1.0x, no acceleration. |\n107\t| Trained dt_emb: accelerating collapse | exp_006 (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=15) | 485→477→360→collapse; v/e: 53.3%@45K (1.61x)→93.3%@55K (2.31x) → immediate collapse |\n108\t| v/e rate comparison (H=8 controlled) | exp_008c vs exp_008d | 008c (untrained): @35K=21.8% (0.90x)→@65K=32.8% (0.58x) (normalized declining); 008d (trained): @25K=15.5% (1.78x)→@35K=41.2% (1.71x) (normalized >1.5x = pathological). ~7x slower overestimation rate |\n109\t| Baseline v/e calibration | exp_001_baseline | v/e: 8.7%@25K → 24.1%@35K → 40.4%@55K → 56.3%@65K → 65.6%@105K (stable 69-72% from 135K+). 008a normalized v/e: 0.93x@55K → 0.66x@65K → 0.59x@105K (suppressed, diverging further below baseline); 008d: 1.7x@35K → 2.1x@45K (optimistic drift) |\n110\t\n111\t**Strength**: Strong (B+ confirmed with 105K long-run data). Two complete zigzag recovery cycles (501→608→470→778) with normalized v/e consistently 0.59-0.93x baseline across 105K steps. This is structural value growth suppression, not a transient delay. Contrasts sharply with exp_006's accelerating v/e (1.6x→2.3x baseline → collapse). The mechanism is clear: untrained dt_embedding prevents the value function from exploiting precise (but wrong) dt>1 predictions, suppressing the optimistic value drift that drives the CIVO feedback loop in trained variants.\n112\t\n113\t**Diagnostic metric: v/e overshoot ratio** = experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same training step. >1.5x = pathological warning. Trained dt_emb variants: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x. Untrained: 008a normalized v/e range 0.59-0.93x over 55K-105K (consistently below baseline = healthy, diverging further from baseline over time).\n114\t\n115\t**105K long-run confirmed**: exp_008a ran to 112K steps. Normalized v/e at 105K=0.59x baseline (lowest point), confirming value growth suppression is structural, not a transient delay. The suppression strengthens over time rather than converging to baseline levels.\n116\t\n117\t### C4: Longer dt amplifies the problem\n118\t\n119\tIncreasing dt from 2 to 4 worsens degradation, consistent with RSSM prediction error compounding over longer temporal gaps.\n120\t\n121\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n122\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n123\t| dt=2 degradation | exp_006_fixed_dt2 | Peak 485, collapse to ~135 |\n124\t| dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n125\t| Monotonic relationship | dt=1 (~950) > dt=2 (peak 485@35K) > dt=4 (peak 177@25K) | Clear monotonic decline |\n126\t\n127\t**Strength**: Moderate. Only two dt values tested (2 and 4). The monotonic trend is clear but a finer grid (dt=3, dt=6) would strengthen this claim. Sufficient for a supporting claim but not a standalone finding.\n128\t\n129\t### C5: Learning signal reduction from shorter effective horizons compounds degradation but is not the root cause\n130\t\n131\tSubsampling with dt>1 reduces the effective number of learning transitions per episode. This contributes to degradation but is neither necessary nor sufficient for collapse.\n132\t\n133\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n134\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n135\t| Subsample OFF, still degraded | exp_008c (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval ~242 vs baseline ~950 |\n136\t| Subsample OFF, no collapse | exp_008a (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | eval 778@105K (85% baseline), no collapse through 112K steps |\n137\t| Subsample ON, collapse | exp_008d (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval 146, value overestimation |\n138\t| Horizon effect | exp_008c (H=8) vs exp_008a (H=15) | H=15 significantly outperforms H=8, both subsample OFF |\n139\t| Subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, no collapse | exp_008e_v3 (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval 382@45K (86% baseline); normalized v/e 0.88x@45K (normal). Even with trained dt_emb and subsample ON, restricting imagination to dt=1 prevents collapse |\n140\t\n141\t**Strength**: Moderate. The 008c vs 008a comparison shows horizon length matters independently. exp_008e_v3 confirms subsample ON does not trigger collapse when imagination is restricted to dt=1, even with trained dt_emb. But disentangling horizon from subsample effects requires additional controls. The key point—that it's not the root cause—is well-supported by 008c's stability despite lower performance.\n142\t\n143\t### C6: dt=1 imagination delays but does not eliminate CIVO collapse — the vulnerability is the trained dt_embedding itself\n144\t\n145\t**⚠️ D038 重大修正**: 008e_v3_s2 (seed=2) **崩溃** — eval 715@95K → 452 → 137@105K, nve=3.78x@105K, 全程 gate_dt_mean=1.0（dt=1 imagination 从未违反）。\n146\t\n147\t原假设\"dt=1 imagination = practical mitigation\"被**否证**。跨 seed 数据：\n148\t- seed 1: eval=869.9@105K ✓（95% baseline, nve=0.80x）\n149\t- seed 2: eval=137@105K ✗（崩溃, nve=3.78x）\n150\t- **独立 seed 崩溃率: 1/2 (50%)**\n151\t\n152\t对比 008d (trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination): 同样 1/2 崩溃率（seed 1 collapse, seed 2 self-correct@268）。dt=1 imagination **未降低崩溃概率**。\n153\t\n154\t**⚠️ D039 机制辨析 — 008e_v3_s2 的崩溃可能不是 CIVO**\n155\t\n156\tReviewer 深度分析揭示 008e_v3_s2 与 008d 的崩溃动力学**定性不同**：\n157\t\n158\t| 特征 | 008d s1 (经典 CIVO) | 008e_v3_s2 (待确认) |\n159\t|------|---------------------|---------------------|\n160\t| value 变化 | 爆炸 (60→138, +130%@55K) | 温和 (339→384→340, +13%→-12%) |\n161\t| eval 变化 | 后跌 (146→83, -43%) | **先跌** (715→452→137, -81%) |\n162\t| nve 驱动 | value 分子驱动（value 爆炸） | eval 分母驱动（eval 崩溃） |\n163\t| actor_entropy | ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) 策略变确定 | **↑** (-5.21→-3.83) 策略变随机 |\n164\t| 机制 | critic overestimate → actor exploit | eval crash → value 滞后跟随 |\n165\t\n166\t**结论**: 008e_v3_s2 的 nve=3.78x 是 eval 崩溃的**结果**，不是 value overestimation 的**原因**。这更像 policy instability 而非 CIVO。\n167\t\n168\t**D040 最终判定**: 009a baseline seed 2 在 85-105K **完全稳定** (618→596→836, 正常波动后恢复)。排除 \"seed 2 本身不稳定\" 假说。**008e_v3_s2 的崩溃是配置导致的**。\n169\t\n170\t| Step | baseline s2 (009a) | 008e_v3 s2 | 差异 |\n171\t|------|-------------------|------------|------|\n172\t| 85K | 618.7 | 715.2 | 008e_v3 略高 |\n173\t| 95K | 596.2 (↓3.6%) | 452.4 (**↓37%**) | 10× 更大跌幅 |\n174\t| 105K | 836.5 (↑40%) | 137.0 (**↓70%**) | baseline 恢复 vs 008e_v3 继续崩 |\n175\t\n176\t**C6 最终状态: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED**\n177\t- dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃（008e_v3: 1/2 seeds 崩溃 vs baseline: 0/2 seeds 崩溃）\n178\t- 崩溃是配置导致的，不是 seed 特异性\n179\t- 但崩溃**机制**与经典 CIVO 不同（见上方对比表）→ trained dt_embedding 可能通过**多条路径**创造脆弱性\n180\t\n181\t**论文表述建议**: \"Restricting imagination to dt=1 does not prevent collapse in the presence of trained dt-embedding (1/2 seeds collapse). While the collapse shares the high nve signature with classic CIVO, the underlying dynamics differ — the collapsed seed exhibits eval-driven destabilization with increasing policy entropy, rather than value-driven exploitation with decreasing entropy. This suggests trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through the value overestimation feedback loop.\"\n182\t\n183\t| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n184\t|----------|-----------|------------|\n185\t| dt=1 imag, seed 1, healthy | exp_008e_v3_walker_s1 (trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval=869.9@105K (95% baseline), nve=0.80x |\n186\t| **dt=1 imag, seed 2, COLLAPSED** | exp_008e_v3_walker_s2 (same config, seed=2) | eval 715@95K → 137@105K, nve ≤0.68x@85K → 1.34x@95K → **3.78x@105K**. gate_dt=1.0 throughout |\n187\t| dt=2 imag, seed 1, collapsed | exp_008d (trained dt_emb, dt=2 imag, H=8) | eval 167→82@55K, nve 1.78x@25K → 2.09x@45K. Onset at 25K |\n188\t| dt=2 imag, seed 2, self-corrected | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 268@105K (rising), nve peak 1.62x@45K → self-correction |\n189\t| Onset delay | 008e_v3_s2 vs 008d | 008d onset nve>1.5x at 25-35K; 008e_v3_s2 at 95-105K. Delay ~70K steps |\n190\t| No dt_emb, stable | exp_008f (dt_embed_dim=0, subsample ON) | No collapse, reward_MAE 75x baseline |\n191\t| Untrained dt_emb, stable | exp_008a (untrained, subsample OFF, H=15) | 778@105K (85% baseline), nve 0.59-0.93x |\n192\t\n193\t**Strength**: **Moderate (D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED)** — dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃 (1/2 seeds)，但崩溃机制与经典 CIVO 不同 (eval-driven vs value-driven)。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱假说。核心洞察：trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways — CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) 和 non-CIVO policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2)。dt=1 imagination 消除 Pathway A 但不消除 Pathway B。\n194\t\n195\t---\n196\t\n197\t## Story Arc\n198\t\n199\t### 1. Introduction\n200\t- Temporal abstraction is a long-sought goal in RL (options, macro-actions, skip connections)\n201\t- World-model RL (DreamerV3) offers a natural testbed: modify imagination dt without changing environment interaction\n202\t- Puzzle: all our attempts at variable-rate imagination degrade performance. Why?\n203\t- Contribution: systematic factorial diagnosis identifying trained dt_embedding as the reliable trigger for conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO, nve>1.5x), with collapse as a stochastic consequence. The vulnerability is deeper than imagination quality — even dt=1 imagination collapses at the same rate when dt_embedding is trained, revealing that training data dt>1 transitions alone drive the critic exploitation feedback loop\n204\t\n205\t### 2. Background & Related Work\n206\t- Temporal abstraction in model-free RL (options framework, FiGAR, action repeat)\n207\t- World-model RL (Dreamer family, IRIS, TWM)\n208\t- Variable-rate planning in world models (few prior works, mostly positive framing)\n209\t- Value overestimation in model-based RL (delusional bias per Lu et al. 2018 — differentiate from our CIVO)\n210\t\n211\t### 3. Methodology\n212\t- DreamerV3 as base architecture\n213\t- Variable-rate imagination: dt_embedding, temporal subsampling, learned gating\n214\t- **Factorial experiment design**: 2×2×2 grid (dt_embedding trained/untrained × subsample ON/OFF × H=8/15) + baselines\n215\t- Diagnostic metrics: eval return, value/eval ratio as overestimation proxy, learning curves\n216\t\n217\t### 4. Results\n218\t- **Finding 1**: All dt>1 configurations degrade (C1)\n219\t- **Finding 2**: Controlled ablation isolates trained dt_embedding (C2) — the \"smoking gun\" comparison (008c vs 008d)\n220\t- **Finding 3**: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, sustaining near-baseline performance (C3)\n221\t- **Finding 4**: Longer dt amplifies (C4)\n222\t- **Finding 5**: Horizon/subsample effects are secondary (C5)\n223\t- **Finding 6 (partially falsified)**: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse (1/2 seeds), but collapsed seed (008e_v3_s2) shows non-CIVO dynamics (eval-driven, entropy ↑) unlike 008d's classic CIVO (value-driven, entropy ↓). 009a baseline s2 stable → collapse is config-caused. Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through value overestimation (C6, D040)\n224\t\n225\t### 5. Discussion\n226\t- Mechanism: why trained dt_embedding causes CIVO (RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1)\n227\t- RSSM fidelity analysis: 008d reward gap (2.78x) vs 008f reward gap (1.01x) — trained dt_emb 让 reward head 在 dt=2 时产生系统性偏差，这是 critic 被误导的直接来源\n228\t- 008f 的 reward 退化 trade-off：去掉 dt_embedding 避免崩塌但牺牲 reward 预测质量\n229\t- **D040: Multiple vulnerability pathways**: 008e_v3_s2 在 dt=1 imagination 下崩溃 (nve=3.78x@105K)，但机制与 008d 的 CIVO 不同：008d = value explosion → eval decline → entropy ↓；008e_v3_s2 = eval crash → value stable → entropy ↑。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱。Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways: Pathway A (CIVO, value-driven) requires dt>1 imagination; Pathway B (non-CIVO policy instability, eval-driven) operates independently of imagination dt\n230\t- Connection to existing theory: non-delusional Q-learning (Lu et al., 2018), model exploitation in MBRL\n231\t- Why this is specific to world-model RL (model-free temporal abstraction doesn't suffer the same failure mode)\n232\t- **D032: Value self-correction mechanism (009b)**: 009b (008d seed 2) demonstrates that pathological overestimation (nve>1.5x) is not always irreversible. Value peaked at 110.9@65K then monotonically self-corrected to 67.3@105K, while eval simultaneously improved (172→268). Hypothesized mechanism: when the critic's overestimation does not yet dominate the actor's gradient signal (transient vs sustained nve>1.5x), the actor can still learn from real environment returns, creating a corrective force. In 008d seed 1, the sustained overestimation (3 consecutive checkpoints >1.5x at 35K+) overwhelmed this correction. The bifurcation likely depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and data sampling order determine whether the overestimation feedback loop locks in before correction can take hold (consistent with primacy bias in MBRL, Qiao et al. 2023; early outlier runs, Bjorck et al. 2022). This makes nve>1.5x a **necessary condition** for collapse but not sufficient — the temporal persistence of overestimation is the second gate.\n233\t\n234\t### 6. Implications for Future Work\n235\t- **D038 update**: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient — trained dt_embedding in critic is the fundamental vulnerability, regardless of imagination strategy\n236\t- Don't use trained dt_embedding for temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training without safeguards (TAWM shows this works in MPC — the vulnerability is specific to imagination-based training)\n237\t- RSSM prediction quality must improve before variable-rate imagination is viable\n238\t- Potential safeguards: (a) ~~detach dt_embedding gradients from reward head~~ **架构层面无效（reward gradient = 0 by design）。梯度分解揭示 dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward 贡献为 0。真正的干预目标是 observation decoder→dt_emb 梯度通路（Full-SGS, exp_018 planned）。**, (b) stop-gradient on dt>1 critic targets, (c) pessimistic value ensemble for dt>1 transitions, (d) uncertainty-gated truncation\n239\t- Need for diagnostic methodology in temporal abstraction research\n240\t\n241\t---\n242\t\n243\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n244\t\n245\t### R1: \"Limited to one environment / one base algorithm\"\n246\t**Pre-emption**: Our claims are mechanistic (trained dt_embedding → value overestimation → collapse). The causal chain is architecture-specific by design—we diagnose *why* it fails in DreamerV3, not whether it fails everywhere. The mechanism (RSSM fidelity gap) likely generalizes to other latent-dynamics models but we scope claims conservatively.\n247\t**Mitigation**: Cheetah Run is a planned experiment in our experiment schedule, not a stretch goal. We will include at least one additional DMC environment (Cheetah Run) to demonstrate generality of the mechanism.\n248\t\n249\t### R2: \"No proposed solution — what's the actionable takeaway?\"\n250\t**Pre-emption**: Our primary contribution is diagnostic, not prescriptive. The causal chain (C1-C5) provides clear guidance: trained dt_embedding is the necessary condition for CIVO. Restricting imagination to dt=1 eliminates CIVO (Pathway A) but not all failure modes — 008e_v3 shows 1/2 seeds collapse via a distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven policy instability). The actionable takeaway is that trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways in imagination-based actor-critic; surface-level fixes are insufficient. Hierarchical approaches (THICK, Hieros) that avoid flat dt-conditioning are the recommended direction.\n251\t**Mitigation**: Present the dual-pathway finding as a key diagnostic contribution (Section 5). dt=1 imagination is a partial mitigation (eliminates CIVO) but not robust (1/2 collapse via Pathway B).\n252\t\n253\t### R3: \"Value/eval ratio is a crude proxy for overestimation\"\n254\t**Pre-emption**: We acknowledge this is a proxy, not a formal divergence measure. However, the v/e overshoot ratio (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) consistently identifies pathological overestimation: trained dt_emb variants exceed 1.5x baseline v/e before collapse, while untrained variants stay at or below baseline levels.\n255\t**Mitigation**: Add distributional analysis of value predictions vs. realized returns if time permits.\n256\t\n257\t### R4: \"The factorial design is incomplete — missing cells in the grid\"\n258\t**Pre-emption**: We prioritized the most informative comparisons. The 008c vs 008d pair isolates dt_embedding training with all else held constant—this is the critical controlled experiment.\n259\t**Mitigation**: Explicitly present the factorial grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify the prioritization.\n260\t\n261\t### R5: \"Novelty concern — model exploitation / value overestimation in MBRL is well-known\"\n262\t**Pre-emption**: General MBRL overestimation is known (Janner et al., 2019), but the specific mechanism through temporal abstraction's dt_embedding is novel. Prior work on temporal abstraction in world models has not identified this failure mode. Our contribution is the *specific causal link*, not the general phenomenon.\n263\t\n264\t### R6: \"All results from a single seed — how robust are the conclusions?\"\n265\t**Pre-emption (D032 update)**: Multi-seed data now available. The nve>1.5x diagnostic threshold achieves **4/4 perfect classification** across seeds: trained dt_emb (008d seed 1, 009b seed 2) both exceed 1.5x; untrained (008f seed 1, 009c seed 2) neither exceeds 1.5x. Collapse itself is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds), which we report transparently — our claim is about the reliable **diagnostic marker** (nve>1.5x), not deterministic collapse.\n266\t**[D040]** C6 partially falsified: 008e_v3_s2 collapsed via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable (618→596→836) — rules out seed fragility. Trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways: CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) and policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2). dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A but not B.\n267\t**Mitigation**: Report all seeds with IQM + confidence intervals per Agarwal et al. (2021). The 4/4 separation is robust. Additional seeds (009e, 009f) pending to further narrow collapse probability estimates.\n268\t\n269\t### R7: \"The factorial design confounds dt_embedding training with training data composition\"\n270\t**Pre-emption**: exp_008f confirmed: dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON → normalized v/e 0.83x@45K (healthy), vs 008d@45K=2.09x (collapse at v/e=69.3%). This confirms dt_embedding conditioning—not RSSM learned dynamics—is the causal mechanism. exp_008f uses identical training data composition as 008d (subsample ON, dt=2 transitions) but removes dt_embedding conditioning. Through 55K, 008f remained in normal range (normalized v/e 0.73-1.02x).\n271\t**Mitigation**: The 008f causal control resolves this confound definitively. The three-way comparison (008c: subsample OFF/untrained, 008d: subsample ON/trained, 008f: subsample ON/no dt_embed) isolates dt_embedding conditioning as the necessary and sufficient factor for CIVO.\n272\t\n273\t### R8: \"008f reward prediction degradation undermines the causal control\"\n274\t**Pre-emption**: 008f 的 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 75x）是重要发现，但不削弱 C2 因果结论。008f 证明的是\"去掉 dt_embedding → 不崩塌\"，这个 collapse vs no-collapse 的区分不依赖 reward 质量。008f 的低 v/e 部分源于奖励噪声，但关键对比是 008d 的 v/e 加速发散 vs 008f 的 v/e 保持稳定——模式差异才是因果证据。\n275\t**Mitigation**: 论文中明确区分 008f 的角色（因果控制实验）和 008e_v3 的角色（诊断证据：dt=1 imagination 不能消除 CIVO）。RSSM fidelity 表同时报告绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被 008d 的低 obs gap ratio（1.30x，但绝对误差 41.31 已是 baseline 3x）误导。\n276\t\n277\t---\n278\t\n279\t## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n280\t\n281\t| Claim | Evidence Status | Gaps |\n282\t|-------|----------------|------|\n283\t| C1 (degradation) | ✅ Strong — 3 configurations (005, 006, 007) all negative | Could add 1 more environment |\n284\t| C2 (reliable pathological overestimation) | ⚠️ Moderate-strong (D032) — nve>1.5x threshold: 4/4 perfect separation (trained 2/2 exceed, untrained 0/2 exceed). Collapse stochastic: 1/2 trained seeds. Causal direction unambiguous. | 009e (008d seed 1) to narrow collapse probability; 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c→200K |\n285\t| C3 (preventing optimistic drift) | ✅ Strong (105K confirmed) — 008a@105K=778 (85% baseline), normalized v/e=0.59x, two V-shaped recoveries | None — long-term structural suppression confirmed |\n286\t| C4 (dt amplification) | ⚠️ Moderate — only dt=2 vs dt=4 | Would benefit from dt=3, but not blocking |\n287\t| C5 (horizon/subsample) | ⚠️ Moderate — confounded with other factors | Need 008c-equivalent with H=15 + subsample ON + untrained dt_emb for clean isolation |\n288\t| C6 (dt=1 imag as mitigation) | ⚠️ **D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED** — s2 collapsed (nve=3.78x) but via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable → config-caused. **Repositioned**: trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways; dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO pathway but not policy instability pathway. Onset delay ~70K steps is a positive finding. | Need s3-s4 to narrow collapse rate. Characterize Pathway B mechanism. Consider: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag (no dt>1 in training data) |\n289\t\n290\t### Priority Actions Before Submission\n291\t1. ~~**Check exp_008a@65K**~~ — ✅ Done: 608 (-1.3% baseline), B+ confirmed\n292\t2. ~~**008e_v3 B+ confound**~~ — ✅ Done: 869.9@105K (95% baseline), B+ confirmed\n293\t3. ~~**008f C2 causal control**~~ — ✅ Done: V-recovery to 503@105K, normalized v/e=0.30x\n294\t4. ~~**008e_v3_s2 (seed=2)**~~ — ❌ D040: **COLLAPSED via non-CIVO pathway** (eval-driven, entropy ↑). C6 partially falsified — multiple vulnerability pathways.\n295\t5. ~~**Multi-seed replication**~~ — ✅ D040: 009a baseline s2 DONE (stable). All Walker Walk seeds complete.\n296\t6. ~~**Narrative pivot**~~ — ✅ D040: C6 reframed as \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B)\n297\t6.5. ~~**SGS (reward_dt_emb_detach) 验证**~~ — ❌ exp_016e: reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op（梯度验证揭示 reward gradient = 0 by architecture）。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异，非 SGS 效果。dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。\n298\t7. **考虑新实验**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag — 如果 stable → 确认 training data dt>1 transitions 是 trigger\n299\t8. **Cheetah Run cross-environment** — P0 priority, addresses R1\n300\t9. **Figures** — learning curves, normalized v/e (含 008e_v3_s2 崩溃轨迹), RSSM fidelity table\n301\t10. **Threshold sensitivity analysis** — plan_034, 0 GPU cost\n302\t\n303\t---\n304\t\n305\t## Key Figures (Planned)\n306\t\n307\t1. **Figure 1**: Learning curves — all experiments overlaid, showing degradation pattern (hero figure)\n308\t2. **Figure 2**: Value/eval ratio over training — separating healthy vs. pathological regimes\n309\t3. **Figure 3**: Factorial grid summary — 2×2 (dt_emb × subsample) with H as secondary axis\n310\t4. **Figure 4**: Causal diagram — dt_embedding → value overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n311\t5. **Figure 5**: dt=1 vs dt=2 vs dt=4 comparison — monotonic degradation (C4)\n312\t6. **Table 1**: RSSM fidelity — absolute obs_MSE + reward_MAE at dt=1/dt=2, 三模型并排（绝对值 + 比率，避免比率误导）\n313\t7. **Figure 6 (D035)**: Mitigation comparison — plot 008e_v3 seed=1 as single line. Add s2 (seed=2) when done. Do NOT present s0+s1 as two independent seeds. Can footnote deterministic reproduction.\n314\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d55a81c1-4c5b-4fe3-9489-00d25d47d0f7","uuid":"824fc1f3-65fa-40cb-874f-8ff1b81628de","timestamp":"2026-05-16T14:02:15.847Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T133210/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing: Diagnostic Analysis of Temporal Abstraction in World-Model RL\n\n## Title Candidates\n\n1. **\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate Imagination\"**\n2. ~~**\"The Delusional Critic Problem in DreamerV3's Variable-Rate Imagination\"**~~ (deprecated — \"delusional critic\" conflicts with Lu et al. 2018)\n3. **\"Diagnosing Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3\"** ← preferred after D037 terminology decision\n4. **\"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"**\n\n**⚠️ D037 术语决定**: \"delusional critic\" → **\"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"** (简称 CIVO)。Lu et al. 2018 的 delusional bias 指 policy-class constraints 导致不可实现的 Q 值，本文机制是 model imprecision + learned conditioning amplification，概念不同。Dream 模拟三档审稿一致指出术语误导。全文替换：\"delusional critic\" → \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\"，可 cite Lu et al. 2018 as analogy but must explicitly differentiate。\n\n**⚠️ D037 TAWM 对比（关键 novelty 升级）**: TAWM (Nhu et al., ICML 2025) 在 TD-MPC2 (MPC setting) 中成功使用 dt-conditioning。本文发现 dt-conditioning 在 imagination-based actor-critic 中失败 → **novelty 从 \"known failure class 的 specific instance\" 升级为 \"dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination — here's the architecture-specific vulnerability\"**。TAWM 是强化 novelty 的最关键参考文献。\n\n## Venue\n\n**CoRL 2026** — Deadline: 2026-05-29\n\n## Narrative Positioning\n\nThis is a **diagnostic analysis paper**, not a method paper. We do not propose a new algorithm. Instead, we provide a systematic, controlled investigation answering: *Why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?*\n\nThe contribution is a **strongly supported causal chain** from mechanism to failure mode, backed by a factorial experiment design that isolates each factor, plus a **practical mitigation guideline** (restrict imagination to dt=1 during multi-dt training → 95% baseline recovery). Diagnostic papers are valued when they (a) address a widely-attempted but poorly-understood failure, (b) provide actionable insight for future method design, and (c) are methodologically rigorous.\n\nWe scope all claims to **DreamerV3 and its RSSM world model**. Generalizability to other latent-dynamics architectures is discussed but not claimed.\n\n---\n\n## Core Claims\n\n### C1: Variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3 consistently degrades performance\n\nTemporal abstraction via variable dt—whether learned or fixed—degrades asymptotic performance relative to the dt=1 baseline across all tested configurations.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Baseline stability | exp_001_baseline (dt=1) | Stable convergence ~950 eval return |\n| Fixed dt=2 collapse | exp_006_fixed_dt2 (dt=2, subsample ON, H=15) | Peak 485@35K → collapse to ~135@65K |\n| Fixed dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 (dt=4) | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n| Learned gate collapse | exp_005 (learned gate) | Peak 541@55K → 99@145K |\n\n**Strength**: Strong. Three independent dt>1 configurations all show degradation. No positive result exists for variable-rate imagination in our setup.\n\n### C2: Trained dt_embedding reliably triggers pathological value overestimation (>1.5x), creating high collapse risk\n\n**D032 update: nve>1.5x threshold has perfect separating power (4/4); collapse itself is stochastic.**\n\nThe trained dt_embedding reliably induces pathological value overestimation (normalized v/e >1.5x baseline) across all tested seeds, but whether this overestimation progresses to catastrophic performance collapse is stochastic:\n\n- **WITH trained dt_emb**: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (008d seed 1: sustained 1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K → collapse; 009b seed 2: 1.62x@45K → self-correction to 0.38x@105K)\n- **WITHOUT dt_emb (008f)**: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x at 35K+ (seed 1 peak 1.02x@25K; seed 2 peak 1.26x@25K)\n- **1.5x threshold (35K+)**: 4/4 perfect classification — reliable diagnostic marker separating trained vs untrained dt_emb\n\n**⚠️ 时间限定**: nve>1.5x 诊断适用于 **35K+ steps**（此时 baseline v/e 已充分收敛至 >0.24，比率稳定可靠）。25K 处 baseline v/e 仅 0.087，微小绝对差异可被放大为大比率（如 009b@25K nve=2.05x 和 008e_v3@25K nve=1.93x 均为 false positive）。论文中报告 threshold 时必须标注此限定。\n\n**Sustained vs transient overestimation**: The key difference between collapse (008d seed 1) and self-correction (009b seed 2) is the **temporal persistence** of nve>1.5x. Seed 1 maintained nve>1.5x across 3 consecutive checkpoints at 35K+ (1.71x@35K→2.09x@45K→4.12x@55K, accelerating), locking the positive feedback loop; seed 2 exceeded 1.5x at 45K (1.62x) but dropped to 1.41x@55K and continued declining, allowing self-correction. This suggests a \"point of no return\" where sustained overestimation becomes irreversible, with early training stochasticity (initialization, data sampling order) determining which path is taken (cf. Henderson et al. 2018, Bjorck et al. 2022, Qiao et al. 2023).\n\nThe trained dt_embedding enables precise dt prediction in imagination, but RSSM state prediction quality at dt>1 is insufficient to support this precision. The result is conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO) that drives actor learning toward incorrect policies. Without dt_embedding conditioning, the overestimation feedback loop is blocked entirely (nve never exceeds 1.5x).\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Untrained dt_emb, no collapse | exp_008c (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval plateau ~242@55K; v/e: @35K=21.8% (0.90x) → @55K=29.1% (0.72x) → @65K=32.8% (0.58x) (declining normalized v/e, ~7x slower than trained) |\n| Trained dt_emb, collapse | exp_008d (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval declining 167→146→141 @25K→35K→45K; v/e: @25K=15.5% (1.78x) → @35K=41.2% (1.71x) → @45K=69.3% (2.09x) (accelerating overestimation) |\n| Both trained dt_emb → accelerating v/e | exp_006 (H=15) vs exp_008d (H=8) | 006: v/e @35K=31.1% (1.29x)→@45K=53.3% (1.61x)→@55K=93.3% (2.31x); 008d: v/e @35K=41.2% (1.71x)→@45K=69.3% (2.09x) — both show accelerating normalized v/e leading to collapse |\n| Baseline v/e calibration (overshoot reference) | exp_001_baseline | v/e@35K=24.1%, @45K=33.1%, @55K=40.4%. Overshoot ratio: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x (all >1.5x = pathological) |\n| **D032: Trained dt_emb seed 2 — pathological but self-correcting** | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 39→141→164→141→172→209→197→236→279→268@105K (**no collapse**, rising). nve: **2.05x@25K**→1.24x→**1.62x@45K**→1.41x→0.94x→0.66x→0.51x→0.43x→**0.38x@105K**. Transient nve>1.5x with self-correction. Value peaked 110.9@65K → self-corrected to 67.3@105K while eval improved. |\n| **1.5x threshold separating power** | 008d+009b vs 008f+009c | WITH trained dt_emb: 2/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008d peak 2.09x sustained, 009b peak 2.05x transient). WITHOUT dt_emb: 0/2 seeds exceed nve>1.5x (008f peak 1.02x, 009c peak 1.26x). **4/4 perfect classification.** |\n| **Causal control: dt_embed_dim=0 (direction confirmed, seed-dependent)** | exp_008f seed 1 + exp_009c seed 2 | **Seed 1**: eval 197→207→194→173→503@105K (zigzag recovery); nve 1.02x→0.73x→0.83x→0.97x→0.30x. **Seed 2**: eval 169→211→239→359→227→191→148@105K (decline after 75K); nve 1.26x→0.79x→0.72x→0.33x→0.60x→0.90x→1.14x. Both vs 008d@45K nve=2.09x: onset delayed 60K+ steps, severity reduced ~2x. **009c extending to 200K to observe if nve crosses 1.5x.** |\n| **Reward SGS = no-op (reward gradient = 0 by architecture)** | exp_016e (reward_dt_emb_detach=True, subsample ON, H=8, seed 1) | Peak eval 326.7@75K → collapse 31.7@95K, NVE=10.42x. 梯度验证揭示 reward loss 对 dt_emb.weight 贡献为 0——reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异。**dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders（orientations/velocity/height），7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。** |\n| RSSM fidelity analysis | baseline / 008d / 008f | See RSSM Fidelity Table below |\n\n**⚠️ 008f 定位：因果控制实验，非推荐方案。** 008f 去掉 dt_embedding 后 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 的 75x），reward head 无法区分 dt=1/dt=2（reward gap 仅 1.01x vs 008d 的 2.78x）。**D030 更新**：008f seed 2 (009c) 在 105K 达到 nve=1.14x（borderline），seed 1 为 0.30x——protection 不 robust。但 008f 两个 seed 的 v/e 增长速度均显著慢于 008d（008d@45K=2.09x vs 008f seed 2@105K=1.14x），确认 dt_embedding 是 primary amplifier。**C2 因果方向成立，但 \"eliminates\" 改为 \"significantly delays and reduces\"。推荐方案仍是 008e_v3（C6）**。\n\n**RSSM Fidelity Table（论文中必须同时报告绝对误差和比率）：**\n\n| Model | dt1 obs_MSE | dt2 obs_MSE | obs gap | dt1 reward_MAE | dt2 reward_MAE | reward gap |\n|-------|------------|------------|---------|---------------|---------------|------------|\n| baseline (exp_001) | 13.49 | 27.50 | **2.04x** | 0.0102 | 0.0159 | 1.56x |\n| 008d (trained dt_emb, collapsed) | 41.31 | 53.67 | **1.30x** | 0.0568 | 0.1579 | **2.78x** |\n| 008f (no dt_emb, stable) | 8.01 | 22.04 | **2.75x** | 0.7469 | 0.7526 | 1.01x |\n\n**解读要点**：008d 的低 obs gap ratio (1.30x) 具有误导性——其 dt=1 绝对误差本身已是 baseline 的 3x（模型已崩塌）。论文中必须并排呈现绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被比率误导。\n\n**dt_emb Gradient Decomposition (D061)**\n\n梯度验证（torch.autograd.grad analysis, commit 5fc8e49）揭示 dt_emb.weight 的梯度来源分布：\n\n| Source | Grad Norm | Fraction |\n|--------|-----------|----------|\n| Observation decoders (orientations, velocity, height) | 1.505 | 93% |\n| KL dynamics | 0.119 | 7% |\n| Cont head | ~0 (included in decoder total) | <1% |\n| **Reward head** | **0.000** | **0%** |\n| **Total** | **1.624** | **100%** |\n\n这意味着：(1) 016e 的 reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op——detach 了一个不存在的梯度通路；(2) dt_emb 的训练几乎完全由 observation reconstruction loss 驱动；(3) Full-SGS（exp_018）移除 observation decoder 梯度后仅保留 7% KL 梯度，是真正的 gradient-level intervention。\n\n**Strength**: Moderate-strong (D032 update: causal direction + diagnostic marker confirmed; collapse stochastic). The **nve>1.5x threshold** achieves 4/4 perfect separation: all trained dt_emb seeds (008d, 009b) exceed 1.5x; no untrained seeds (008f, 009c) exceed 1.5x. This is the paper's strongest quantitative result for C2. **Collapse** is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds): 008d seed 1 shows sustained nve>1.5x → collapse; 009b seed 2 shows transient nve>1.5x → self-correction. The **causal direction** is unambiguous across all 4 data points. Pending: 009e (008d seed 1) to determine collapse probability (1/3 vs 2/3); 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c extension to 200K.\n\n### C3: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, substantively protecting performance\n\nWhen dt_embedding is untrained (random fixed), the value function cannot exploit precise dt information, preventing the optimistic value drift seen in trained variants. This value growth suppression substantively protects performance: exp_008a demonstrates two complete zigzag recovery cycles over 105K steps (peak @105K=778, 85% of baseline 916), with normalized v/e consistently suppressed at 0.59-0.93x baseline. The trained dt_embedding variant (exp_006) collapses catastrophically as its v/e ratio accelerates far past baseline levels (2.3x baseline@55K).\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Two V-shaped recoveries (105K) | exp_008a (dt=2, subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | 501→563→488→608→470→584→572→778; two complete recovery cycles over 105K steps. @65K=608 (-1.3% baseline), @105K=778 (-15.1% baseline, 85% of 916). No collapse. |\n| Sustained v/e suppression | exp_008a | v/e: 37.6%@55K → 37.2%@65K → 53.6%@75K → 47.5%@85K → 49.7%@95K → 38.6%@105K. Normalized v/e: 0.93x→0.66x→0.75x→0.68x→0.79x→0.59x baseline. Consistently below 1.0x, no acceleration. |\n| Trained dt_emb: accelerating collapse | exp_006 (dt=2, subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=15) | 485→477→360→collapse; v/e: 53.3%@45K (1.61x)→93.3%@55K (2.31x) → immediate collapse |\n| v/e rate comparison (H=8 controlled) | exp_008c vs exp_008d | 008c (untrained): @35K=21.8% (0.90x)→@65K=32.8% (0.58x) (normalized declining); 008d (trained): @25K=15.5% (1.78x)→@35K=41.2% (1.71x) (normalized >1.5x = pathological). ~7x slower overestimation rate |\n| Baseline v/e calibration | exp_001_baseline | v/e: 8.7%@25K → 24.1%@35K → 40.4%@55K → 56.3%@65K → 65.6%@105K (stable 69-72% from 135K+). 008a normalized v/e: 0.93x@55K → 0.66x@65K → 0.59x@105K (suppressed, diverging further below baseline); 008d: 1.7x@35K → 2.1x@45K (optimistic drift) |\n\n**Strength**: Strong (B+ confirmed with 105K long-run data). Two complete zigzag recovery cycles (501→608→470→778) with normalized v/e consistently 0.59-0.93x baseline across 105K steps. This is structural value growth suppression, not a transient delay. Contrasts sharply with exp_006's accelerating v/e (1.6x→2.3x baseline → collapse). The mechanism is clear: untrained dt_embedding prevents the value function from exploiting precise (but wrong) dt>1 predictions, suppressing the optimistic value drift that drives the CIVO feedback loop in trained variants.\n\n**Diagnostic metric: v/e overshoot ratio** = experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same training step. >1.5x = pathological warning. Trained dt_emb variants: 008d@35K=1.7x, @45K=2.1x; 006@45K=1.6x, @55K=2.3x. Untrained: 008a normalized v/e range 0.59-0.93x over 55K-105K (consistently below baseline = healthy, diverging further from baseline over time).\n\n**105K long-run confirmed**: exp_008a ran to 112K steps. Normalized v/e at 105K=0.59x baseline (lowest point), confirming value growth suppression is structural, not a transient delay. The suppression strengthens over time rather than converging to baseline levels.\n\n### C4: Longer dt amplifies the problem\n\nIncreasing dt from 2 to 4 worsens degradation, consistent with RSSM prediction error compounding over longer temporal gaps.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| dt=2 degradation | exp_006_fixed_dt2 | Peak 485, collapse to ~135 |\n| dt=4 severe degradation | exp_007_fixed_dt4 | Peak 176.9@25K, 144.4@45K (33% of baseline@45K=443) |\n| Monotonic relationship | dt=1 (~950) > dt=2 (peak 485@35K) > dt=4 (peak 177@25K) | Clear monotonic decline |\n\n**Strength**: Moderate. Only two dt values tested (2 and 4). The monotonic trend is clear but a finer grid (dt=3, dt=6) would strengthen this claim. Sufficient for a supporting claim but not a standalone finding.\n\n### C5: Learning signal reduction from shorter effective horizons compounds degradation but is not the root cause\n\nSubsampling with dt>1 reduces the effective number of learning transitions per episode. This contributes to degradation but is neither necessary nor sufficient for collapse.\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| Subsample OFF, still degraded | exp_008c (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=8) | eval ~242 vs baseline ~950 |\n| Subsample OFF, no collapse | exp_008a (subsample OFF, untrained dt_emb, H=15) | eval 778@105K (85% baseline), no collapse through 112K steps |\n| Subsample ON, collapse | exp_008d (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, H=8) | eval 146, value overestimation |\n| Horizon effect | exp_008c (H=8) vs exp_008a (H=15) | H=15 significantly outperforms H=8, both subsample OFF |\n| Subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, no collapse | exp_008e_v3 (subsample ON, trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval 382@45K (86% baseline); normalized v/e 0.88x@45K (normal). Even with trained dt_emb and subsample ON, restricting imagination to dt=1 prevents collapse |\n\n**Strength**: Moderate. The 008c vs 008a comparison shows horizon length matters independently. exp_008e_v3 confirms subsample ON does not trigger collapse when imagination is restricted to dt=1, even with trained dt_emb. But disentangling horizon from subsample effects requires additional controls. The key point—that it's not the root cause—is well-supported by 008c's stability despite lower performance.\n\n### C6: dt=1 imagination delays but does not eliminate CIVO collapse — the vulnerability is the trained dt_embedding itself\n\n**⚠️ D038 重大修正**: 008e_v3_s2 (seed=2) **崩溃** — eval 715@95K → 452 → 137@105K, nve=3.78x@105K, 全程 gate_dt_mean=1.0（dt=1 imagination 从未违反）。\n\n原假设\"dt=1 imagination = practical mitigation\"被**否证**。跨 seed 数据：\n- seed 1: eval=869.9@105K ✓（95% baseline, nve=0.80x）\n- seed 2: eval=137@105K ✗（崩溃, nve=3.78x）\n- **独立 seed 崩溃率: 1/2 (50%)**\n\n对比 008d (trained dt_emb + dt=2 imagination): 同样 1/2 崩溃率（seed 1 collapse, seed 2 self-correct@268）。dt=1 imagination **未降低崩溃概率**。\n\n**⚠️ D039 机制辨析 — 008e_v3_s2 的崩溃可能不是 CIVO**\n\nReviewer 深度分析揭示 008e_v3_s2 与 008d 的崩溃动力学**定性不同**：\n\n| 特征 | 008d s1 (经典 CIVO) | 008e_v3_s2 (待确认) |\n|------|---------------------|---------------------|\n| value 变化 | 爆炸 (60→138, +130%@55K) | 温和 (339→384→340, +13%→-12%) |\n| eval 变化 | 后跌 (146→83, -43%) | **先跌** (715→452→137, -81%) |\n| nve 驱动 | value 分子驱动（value 爆炸） | eval 分母驱动（eval 崩溃） |\n| actor_entropy | ↓ (-3.47→-4.88) 策略变确定 | **↑** (-5.21→-3.83) 策略变随机 |\n| 机制 | critic overestimate → actor exploit | eval crash → value 滞后跟随 |\n\n**结论**: 008e_v3_s2 的 nve=3.78x 是 eval 崩溃的**结果**，不是 value overestimation 的**原因**。这更像 policy instability 而非 CIVO。\n\n**D040 最终判定**: 009a baseline seed 2 在 85-105K **完全稳定** (618→596→836, 正常波动后恢复)。排除 \"seed 2 本身不稳定\" 假说。**008e_v3_s2 的崩溃是配置导致的**。\n\n| Step | baseline s2 (009a) | 008e_v3 s2 | 差异 |\n|------|-------------------|------------|------|\n| 85K | 618.7 | 715.2 | 008e_v3 略高 |\n| 95K | 596.2 (↓3.6%) | 452.4 (**↓37%**) | 10× 更大跌幅 |\n| 105K | 836.5 (↑40%) | 137.0 (**↓70%**) | baseline 恢复 vs 008e_v3 继续崩 |\n\n**C6 最终状态: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED**\n- dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃（008e_v3: 1/2 seeds 崩溃 vs baseline: 0/2 seeds 崩溃）\n- 崩溃是配置导致的，不是 seed 特异性\n- 但崩溃**机制**与经典 CIVO 不同（见上方对比表）→ trained dt_embedding 可能通过**多条路径**创造脆弱性\n\n**论文表述建议**: \"Restricting imagination to dt=1 does not prevent collapse in the presence of trained dt-embedding (1/2 seeds collapse). While the collapse shares the high nve signature with classic CIVO, the underlying dynamics differ — the collapsed seed exhibits eval-driven destabilization with increasing policy entropy, rather than value-driven exploitation with decreasing entropy. This suggests trained dt-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through the value overestimation feedback loop.\"\n\n| Evidence | Experiment | Key Metric |\n|----------|-----------|------------|\n| dt=1 imag, seed 1, healthy | exp_008e_v3_walker_s1 (trained dt_emb, dt=1 imag, H=15) | eval=869.9@105K (95% baseline), nve=0.80x |\n| **dt=1 imag, seed 2, COLLAPSED** | exp_008e_v3_walker_s2 (same config, seed=2) | eval 715@95K → 137@105K, nve ≤0.68x@85K → 1.34x@95K → **3.78x@105K**. gate_dt=1.0 throughout |\n| dt=2 imag, seed 1, collapsed | exp_008d (trained dt_emb, dt=2 imag, H=8) | eval 167→82@55K, nve 1.78x@25K → 2.09x@45K. Onset at 25K |\n| dt=2 imag, seed 2, self-corrected | exp_009b (008d config, seed 2) | eval 268@105K (rising), nve peak 1.62x@45K → self-correction |\n| Onset delay | 008e_v3_s2 vs 008d | 008d onset nve>1.5x at 25-35K; 008e_v3_s2 at 95-105K. Delay ~70K steps |\n| No dt_emb, stable | exp_008f (dt_embed_dim=0, subsample ON) | No collapse, reward_MAE 75x baseline |\n| Untrained dt_emb, stable | exp_008a (untrained, subsample OFF, H=15) | 778@105K (85% baseline), nve 0.59-0.93x |\n\n**Strength**: **Moderate (D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED)** — dt=1 imagination 不能阻止崩溃 (1/2 seeds)，但崩溃机制与经典 CIVO 不同 (eval-driven vs value-driven)。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱假说。核心洞察：trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways — CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) 和 non-CIVO policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2)。dt=1 imagination 消除 Pathway A 但不消除 Pathway B。\n\n---\n\n## Story Arc\n\n### 1. Introduction\n- Temporal abstraction is a long-sought goal in RL (options, macro-actions, skip connections)\n- World-model RL (DreamerV3) offers a natural testbed: modify imagination dt without changing environment interaction\n- Puzzle: all our attempts at variable-rate imagination degrade performance. Why?\n- Contribution: systematic factorial diagnosis identifying trained dt_embedding as the reliable trigger for conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO, nve>1.5x), with collapse as a stochastic consequence. The vulnerability is deeper than imagination quality — even dt=1 imagination collapses at the same rate when dt_embedding is trained, revealing that training data dt>1 transitions alone drive the critic exploitation feedback loop\n\n### 2. Background & Related Work\n- Temporal abstraction in model-free RL (options framework, FiGAR, action repeat)\n- World-model RL (Dreamer family, IRIS, TWM)\n- Variable-rate planning in world models (few prior works, mostly positive framing)\n- Value overestimation in model-based RL (delusional bias per Lu et al. 2018 — differentiate from our CIVO)\n\n### 3. Methodology\n- DreamerV3 as base architecture\n- Variable-rate imagination: dt_embedding, temporal subsampling, learned gating\n- **Factorial experiment design**: 2×2×2 grid (dt_embedding trained/untrained × subsample ON/OFF × H=8/15) + baselines\n- Diagnostic metrics: eval return, value/eval ratio as overestimation proxy, learning curves\n\n### 4. Results\n- **Finding 1**: All dt>1 configurations degrade (C1)\n- **Finding 2**: Controlled ablation isolates trained dt_embedding (C2) — the \"smoking gun\" comparison (008c vs 008d)\n- **Finding 3**: Untrained dt_embedding prevents optimistic drift, sustaining near-baseline performance (C3)\n- **Finding 4**: Longer dt amplifies (C4)\n- **Finding 5**: Horizon/subsample effects are secondary (C5)\n- **Finding 6 (partially falsified)**: dt=1 imagination does not prevent collapse (1/2 seeds), but collapsed seed (008e_v3_s2) shows non-CIVO dynamics (eval-driven, entropy ↑) unlike 008d's classic CIVO (value-driven, entropy ↓). 009a baseline s2 stable → collapse is config-caused. Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways, not solely through value overestimation (C6, D040)\n\n### 5. Discussion\n- Mechanism: why trained dt_embedding causes CIVO (RSSM fidelity gap at dt>1)\n- RSSM fidelity analysis: 008d reward gap (2.78x) vs 008f reward gap (1.01x) — trained dt_emb 让 reward head 在 dt=2 时产生系统性偏差，这是 critic 被误导的直接来源\n- 008f 的 reward 退化 trade-off：去掉 dt_embedding 避免崩塌但牺牲 reward 预测质量\n- **D040: Multiple vulnerability pathways**: 008e_v3_s2 在 dt=1 imagination 下崩溃 (nve=3.78x@105K)，但机制与 008d 的 CIVO 不同：008d = value explosion → eval decline → entropy ↓；008e_v3_s2 = eval crash → value stable → entropy ↑。009a baseline s2 稳定排除 seed 脆弱。Trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways: Pathway A (CIVO, value-driven) requires dt>1 imagination; Pathway B (non-CIVO policy instability, eval-driven) operates independently of imagination dt\n- Connection to existing theory: non-delusional Q-learning (Lu et al., 2018), model exploitation in MBRL\n- Why this is specific to world-model RL (model-free temporal abstraction doesn't suffer the same failure mode)\n- **D032: Value self-correction mechanism (009b)**: 009b (008d seed 2) demonstrates that pathological overestimation (nve>1.5x) is not always irreversible. Value peaked at 110.9@65K then monotonically self-corrected to 67.3@105K, while eval simultaneously improved (172→268). Hypothesized mechanism: when the critic's overestimation does not yet dominate the actor's gradient signal (transient vs sustained nve>1.5x), the actor can still learn from real environment returns, creating a corrective force. In 008d seed 1, the sustained overestimation (3 consecutive checkpoints >1.5x at 35K+) overwhelmed this correction. The bifurcation likely depends on early training stochasticity — initialization and data sampling order determine whether the overestimation feedback loop locks in before correction can take hold (consistent with primacy bias in MBRL, Qiao et al. 2023; early outlier runs, Bjorck et al. 2022). This makes nve>1.5x a **necessary condition** for collapse but not sufficient — the temporal persistence of overestimation is the second gate.\n\n### 6. Implications for Future Work\n- **D038 update**: dt=1 imagination is NOT sufficient — trained dt_embedding in critic is the fundamental vulnerability, regardless of imagination strategy\n- Don't use trained dt_embedding for temporal conditioning in imagination-based actor-critic training without safeguards (TAWM shows this works in MPC — the vulnerability is specific to imagination-based training)\n- RSSM prediction quality must improve before variable-rate imagination is viable\n- Potential safeguards: (a) ~~detach dt_embedding gradients from reward head~~ **架构层面无效（reward gradient = 0 by design）。梯度分解揭示 dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward 贡献为 0。真正的干预目标是 observation decoder→dt_emb 梯度通路（Full-SGS, exp_018 planned）。**, (b) stop-gradient on dt>1 critic targets, (c) pessimistic value ensemble for dt>1 transitions, (d) uncertainty-gated truncation\n- Need for diagnostic methodology in temporal abstraction research\n\n---\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n### R1: \"Limited to one environment / one base algorithm\"\n**Pre-emption**: Our claims are mechanistic (trained dt_embedding → value overestimation → collapse). The causal chain is architecture-specific by design—we diagnose *why* it fails in DreamerV3, not whether it fails everywhere. The mechanism (RSSM fidelity gap) likely generalizes to other latent-dynamics models but we scope claims conservatively.\n**Mitigation**: Cheetah Run is a planned experiment in our experiment schedule, not a stretch goal. We will include at least one additional DMC environment (Cheetah Run) to demonstrate generality of the mechanism.\n\n### R2: \"No proposed solution — what's the actionable takeaway?\"\n**Pre-emption**: Our primary contribution is diagnostic, not prescriptive. The causal chain (C1-C5) provides clear guidance: trained dt_embedding is the necessary condition for CIVO. Restricting imagination to dt=1 eliminates CIVO (Pathway A) but not all failure modes — 008e_v3 shows 1/2 seeds collapse via a distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven policy instability). The actionable takeaway is that trained dt_embedding creates vulnerability through multiple pathways in imagination-based actor-critic; surface-level fixes are insufficient. Hierarchical approaches (THICK, Hieros) that avoid flat dt-conditioning are the recommended direction.\n**Mitigation**: Present the dual-pathway finding as a key diagnostic contribution (Section 5). dt=1 imagination is a partial mitigation (eliminates CIVO) but not robust (1/2 collapse via Pathway B).\n\n### R3: \"Value/eval ratio is a crude proxy for overestimation\"\n**Pre-emption**: We acknowledge this is a proxy, not a formal divergence measure. However, the v/e overshoot ratio (experiment v/e / baseline v/e at same step) consistently identifies pathological overestimation: trained dt_emb variants exceed 1.5x baseline v/e before collapse, while untrained variants stay at or below baseline levels.\n**Mitigation**: Add distributional analysis of value predictions vs. realized returns if time permits.\n\n### R4: \"The factorial design is incomplete — missing cells in the grid\"\n**Pre-emption**: We prioritized the most informative comparisons. The 008c vs 008d pair isolates dt_embedding training with all else held constant—this is the critical controlled experiment.\n**Mitigation**: Explicitly present the factorial grid, mark which cells are filled, and justify the prioritization.\n\n### R5: \"Novelty concern — model exploitation / value overestimation in MBRL is well-known\"\n**Pre-emption**: General MBRL overestimation is known (Janner et al., 2019), but the specific mechanism through temporal abstraction's dt_embedding is novel. Prior work on temporal abstraction in world models has not identified this failure mode. Our contribution is the *specific causal link*, not the general phenomenon.\n\n### R6: \"All results from a single seed — how robust are the conclusions?\"\n**Pre-emption (D032 update)**: Multi-seed data now available. The nve>1.5x diagnostic threshold achieves **4/4 perfect classification** across seeds: trained dt_emb (008d seed 1, 009b seed 2) both exceed 1.5x; untrained (008f seed 1, 009c seed 2) neither exceeds 1.5x. Collapse itself is stochastic (1/2 trained seeds), which we report transparently — our claim is about the reliable **diagnostic marker** (nve>1.5x), not deterministic collapse.\n**[D040]** C6 partially falsified: 008e_v3_s2 collapsed via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable (618→596→836) — rules out seed fragility. Trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways: CIVO (Pathway A, 008d) and policy instability (Pathway B, 008e_v3_s2). dt=1 imagination eliminates Pathway A but not B.\n**Mitigation**: Report all seeds with IQM + confidence intervals per Agarwal et al. (2021). The 4/4 separation is robust. Additional seeds (009e, 009f) pending to further narrow collapse probability estimates.\n\n### R7: \"The factorial design confounds dt_embedding training with training data composition\"\n**Pre-emption**: exp_008f confirmed: dt_embed_dim=0 + subsample ON → normalized v/e 0.83x@45K (healthy), vs 008d@45K=2.09x (collapse at v/e=69.3%). This confirms dt_embedding conditioning—not RSSM learned dynamics—is the causal mechanism. exp_008f uses identical training data composition as 008d (subsample ON, dt=2 transitions) but removes dt_embedding conditioning. Through 55K, 008f remained in normal range (normalized v/e 0.73-1.02x).\n**Mitigation**: The 008f causal control resolves this confound definitively. The three-way comparison (008c: subsample OFF/untrained, 008d: subsample ON/trained, 008f: subsample ON/no dt_embed) isolates dt_embedding conditioning as the necessary and sufficient factor for CIVO.\n\n### R8: \"008f reward prediction degradation undermines the causal control\"\n**Pre-emption**: 008f 的 reward_MAE=0.747（baseline 75x）是重要发现，但不削弱 C2 因果结论。008f 证明的是\"去掉 dt_embedding → 不崩塌\"，这个 collapse vs no-collapse 的区分不依赖 reward 质量。008f 的低 v/e 部分源于奖励噪声，但关键对比是 008d 的 v/e 加速发散 vs 008f 的 v/e 保持稳定——模式差异才是因果证据。\n**Mitigation**: 论文中明确区分 008f 的角色（因果控制实验）和 008e_v3 的角色（诊断证据：dt=1 imagination 不能消除 CIVO）。RSSM fidelity 表同时报告绝对误差和比率，避免审稿人被 008d 的低 obs gap ratio（1.30x，但绝对误差 41.31 已是 baseline 3x）误导。\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Claim | Evidence Status | Gaps |\n|-------|----------------|------|\n| C1 (degradation) | ✅ Strong — 3 configurations (005, 006, 007) all negative | Could add 1 more environment |\n| C2 (reliable pathological overestimation) | ⚠️ Moderate-strong (D032) — nve>1.5x threshold: 4/4 perfect separation (trained 2/2 exceed, untrained 0/2 exceed). Collapse stochastic: 1/2 trained seeds. Causal direction unambiguous. | 009e (008d seed 1) to narrow collapse probability; 009f (008f seed 1) for third untrained seed; 009c→200K |\n| C3 (preventing optimistic drift) | ✅ Strong (105K confirmed) — 008a@105K=778 (85% baseline), normalized v/e=0.59x, two V-shaped recoveries | None — long-term structural suppression confirmed |\n| C4 (dt amplification) | ⚠️ Moderate — only dt=2 vs dt=4 | Would benefit from dt=3, but not blocking |\n| C5 (horizon/subsample) | ⚠️ Moderate — confounded with other factors | Need 008c-equivalent with H=15 + subsample ON + untrained dt_emb for clean isolation |\n| C6 (dt=1 imag as mitigation) | ⚠️ **D040: PARTIALLY FALSIFIED** — s2 collapsed (nve=3.78x) but via non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven, entropy ↑). 009a baseline s2 stable → config-caused. **Repositioned**: trained dt_embedding creates multiple vulnerability pathways; dt=1 imagination eliminates CIVO pathway but not policy instability pathway. Onset delay ~70K steps is a positive finding. | Need s3-s4 to narrow collapse rate. Characterize Pathway B mechanism. Consider: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag (no dt>1 in training data) |\n\n### Priority Actions Before Submission\n1. ~~**Check exp_008a@65K**~~ — ✅ Done: 608 (-1.3% baseline), B+ confirmed\n2. ~~**008e_v3 B+ confound**~~ — ✅ Done: 869.9@105K (95% baseline), B+ confirmed\n3. ~~**008f C2 causal control**~~ — ✅ Done: V-recovery to 503@105K, normalized v/e=0.30x\n4. ~~**008e_v3_s2 (seed=2)**~~ — ❌ D040: **COLLAPSED via non-CIVO pathway** (eval-driven, entropy ↑). C6 partially falsified — multiple vulnerability pathways.\n5. ~~**Multi-seed replication**~~ — ✅ D040: 009a baseline s2 DONE (stable). All Walker Walk seeds complete.\n6. ~~**Narrative pivot**~~ — ✅ D040: C6 reframed as \"multiple vulnerability pathways\" (CIVO Pathway A + non-CIVO Pathway B)\n6.5. ~~**SGS (reward_dt_emb_detach) 验证**~~ — ❌ exp_016e: reward_dt_emb_detach 是 no-op（梯度验证揭示 reward gradient = 0 by architecture）。016e 与 008d 的差异（onset timing, peak height）归因于随机变异，非 SGS 效果。dt_emb 的 93% 梯度来自 observation decoders，7% 来自 KL dynamics，reward=0。\n7. **考虑新实验**: trained dt_emb + subsample OFF + dt=1 imag — 如果 stable → 确认 training data dt>1 transitions 是 trigger\n8. **Cheetah Run cross-environment** — P0 priority, addresses R1\n9. **Figures** — learning curves, normalized v/e (含 008e_v3_s2 崩溃轨迹), RSSM fidelity table\n10. **Threshold sensitivity analysis** — plan_034, 0 GPU cost\n\n---\n\n## Key Figures (Planned)\n\n1. **Figure 1**: Learning curves — all experiments overlaid, showing degradation pattern (hero figure)\n2. **Figure 2**: Value/eval ratio over training — separating healthy vs. pathological regimes\n3. **Figure 3**: Factorial grid summary — 2×2 (dt_emb × subsample) with H as secondary axis\n4. **Figure 4**: Causal diagram — dt_embedding → value overestimation → actor degradation → collapse\n5. **Figure 5**: dt=1 vs dt=2 vs dt=4 comparison — monotonic degradation (C4)\n6. **Table 1**: RSSM fidelity — absolute obs_MSE + reward_MAE at dt=1/dt=2, 三模型并排（绝对值 + 比率，避免比率误导）\n7. **Figure 6 (D035)**: Mitigation comparison — plot 008e_v3 seed=1 as single line. Add s2 (seed=2) when done. Do NOT present s0+s1 as two independent seeds. Can footnote deterministic reproduction.\n","numLines":314,"startLine":1,"totalLines":314}}}
